Equation (\ref{eq:SchurComp}) reveals an essential interpretation: 
$\mathbf{S}(\Sigma_2)$ represents the covariance matrix of a level-1 portfolio. This portfolio follows the structure of $G_1$, the leftmost graph in Figure \ref{fig:sierpinski_graphs}, 
and the corresponding generic covariance matrix has the following form,

\begin{align}
\label{eq:Sigma1Generic}
\mathbf{\Sigma}_1 
=
\begin{pmatrix}
