\section*{Methodology}

Suppose \(\mathcal{P} = \{p_t|t=0,1,2,\cdots,T\}\) is the vector of historical prices of agricultural commodities (soybean, rice or cotton). The log-return is defined as
\begin{equation}
   r_t =\ln\Big(\frac{p_t}{p_{t-1}}\Big) = \ln (p_t) - \ln (p_{t-1}).  
\end{equation}

\noindent We  model $r_t$ as mean stationary process with mean as $\mathbb{E}(r_t)=0$ and $\mathbb{V}ar(r_t)=\mathbb{E}(r_t^2) = \sigma_t^2$. \(r_t^2\) is an unbiased estimator of \(\sigma_t^2\) (see Figure~\ref{fig:returns_volatility_subplots}).


\subsection*{EGARCH Model for Conditional Volatility of Price}

The \emph{Exponential Generalised Autoregressive Conditional Heteroskedasticity} (EGARCH) model~\cite{Bollerslev1986,Nelson1991} captures the conditional volatility (\(\sigma_t^2\)) dynamics of the log-returns (\(r_t\)). The EGARCH model is given by:
\begin{equation}
   \ln \sigma_t^2 = \nu + \sum_{i=1}^{p} \kappa_i \left( \left| \frac{\eta_{t-i}}{\sigma_{t-i}} \right| - \sqrt{\frac{2}{\pi}} \right) + \sum_{j=1}^{o} \delta_j \frac{\eta_{t-j}}{\sigma_{t-j}} + \sum_{k=1}^{q} \phi_k \ln \sigma_{t-k}^2. 
\end{equation}

\begin{itemize}[noitemsep]
    \item \(\nu\) represent the constant term. It sets the baseline level of the logarithm of the conditional variance,
    \item \(\eta_t\) denote the innovation (error) term from the mean equation,
    \item \(\kappa_i\) (for \(i=1,\dots,p\)) be the coefficients for the effect of past absolute shocks. It quantifies how the size of past shocks influences current
volatility,
    \item \(\delta_j\) (for \(j=1,\dots,o\)) capture the asymmetric impact of past shocks, allowing the model to treat positive and negative shocks differently,
    \item \(\phi_k\) (for \(k=1,\dots,q\)) model the persistence in volatility by incorporating the influence of past log conditional variances.
    \item \(p\), \(o\), and \(q\) denote the number of lagged terms for the magnitude effect, the asymmetric effect, and the persistence effect, respectively.
\end{itemize}

Figure~\ref{fig:returns_volatility_subplots} shows the conditional volatility estimates.

