A natural problem can be posed as follows: Which is the smallest  connected finite $T_0$-space that is non-contractible and satisfies the condition that its determinant is equal to zero? If we remove the condition of connectedness, it is simple to find an answer to this question.
\begin{prop}\label{prop:pequeno} Let $X=\{x_1,x_2,x_3,x_4\}$ be the finite minimal model of the circle and let $Y=\{y \}$ be the poset of one point. Then the disjoint union $X\sqcup Y$ is the smallest finite $T_0$-space that satisfies $\det(X\sqcup Y)=0$ and is not homotopically trivial.
\end{prop}
\begin{proof}
The smallest non-trivial finite $T_0$-space is $X$. Moreover, $\det(X)=1$, but an easy computation shows that $\det(X\sqcup Y)=0$. 
\end{proof}




