\subsection{Rank}
Consider $X$ to be a finite $T_0$-space. Our focus is to investigate the relationship between the rank of $X_M$, denoted as $\text{rank}(X)$, and the homotopy type of $X$. For this purpose, we introduce a new term, $\overline{\text{rank}}(X)$, which is defined as the difference between the cardinality of $X$ and the rank of $X$, i.e., $\overline{\text{rank}}(X)=|X|-\text{rank}(X)$.

\begin{thm} Let $X$ and $Y$ be finite $T_0$-spaces. If $X$ is homotopy equivalent to $Y$, then $\overline{\textnormal{rank}}(X)=\overline{\textnormal{rank}}(Y)$.
\end{thm}  
\begin{proof}
Assume that $X$ and $Y$ are homotopy equivalent. Consequently, their cores are homeomorphic. This implies that $X_M$ and $Y_M$ after performing certain row and column operations can be respectively represented as
$$\begin{pmatrix}
I_n & 0\\
0 & \overline{X}_M
\end{pmatrix}\quad \quad \quad \begin{pmatrix}
I_m & 0\\
0 & \overline{Y}_M,
\end{pmatrix}$$  
where $\overline{X}_M$ ($\overline{Y}_M$) denotes the matrix of the core of $X$ ($Y$), and $\overline{X}_M$ and $\overline{Y}_M$ are equivalent matrices (see Theorem \ref{thm:stong_cores_homeomorphic}). Therefore, we have $|X|-\text{rank}(X)=|Y|-\text{rank}(Y)$. 
\end{proof}
