\begin{prop}
Let $X$ be a finite $T_0$-space that is simple homotopy equivalent to a finite $T_0$-space of height $1$. Then $|\det (X)|=-\chi (X)+1$.
\end{prop}
\begin{proof}
We may suppose that $X$ has height one because $|\det (X)|$ is a simple homotopy invariant. Since $X$ has height one, we get that $X$ is simple homotopy equivalent to $[2]\circledast [n]$ for some $n\geq 1$. Thus, $|\det (X)|=n-1$ and $|\det (X)|=-\chi(X)+1$.
\end{proof}
Hence, $|\det(\cdot)|$ classifies the simple homotopy type of finite spaces of height one.

\begin{rem}\label{rem:esferas} Notice that the minimal finite model of the $n$-dimensional sphere may be obtained as $[2]\circledast \cdots^n \circledast [2]$. Enumerating the points from bottom to top, we get that the resulting matrix is a block matrix $\{A_{i,j} \}_{i,j=1,...,n+1}$ given by $A_{i,j}=1_{2\times 2}$ if $j<i$, $A_{i,j}=0_{2\times 2}$ if $i<j$ and $A_{i,i}=1_{2\times 2}-I_{2\times 2}$. From this, it is simple to deduce that the inverse of this matrix is given by the block matrix $\{B_{i,j}\}_{i,j=1,...,n+1}$ defined by $B_{i,j}=0_{2\times 2}$ if $i<j$, $B_{i,j}=(-1)^{i+j}1_{2\times 2}$ if $j<i$ and $B_{i,i}=1_{2\times 2}-I_{2\times 2}$.
\end{rem}

\subsection{Product}

Let $X$ and $Y$ be finite $T_0$-spaces. The Cartesian ordering of $X\times Y$ is given by $(x,y)\leq (x',y')$ if and only if $x\leq x'$ and $y\leq y'$.  Recall that $|\mathcal{K}(X\times Y)|$ is homeomorphic to  $|\mathcal{K}(X)|\times |\mathcal{K}(Y)|$.
\begin{thm}[Product]\label{prop:product} Let $X$ and $Y$ be finite $T_0$-spaces such that $|X|=n$ and $|Y|=m$. Then $(X\times Y)_M$ is $X_M\otimes Y_M$, where $\otimes$ denotes the Kronecker product and we use the following rule: $x_{ij}Y_M=Y_M$ if $x_{ij}=0$ and $x_{ij}Y_M=1_{m\times m}$ if $x_{ij}=1$.
\end{thm}
\begin{proof}
Consider $X\times Y=\{z_i \}_{i=1,...,nm}$ satisfying that $z_k=(x_i,y_j)$ where $(i-1)m< k\leq im $ and $j=k-(i-1)m$ for some $i=1,...,n$. The block $A_{i,j}=\{z_{kl}\}$ (of dimension $m \times m$) of $(X\times Y)_M$ with $(i-1)m< k\leq im$ and $(j-1)m< l\leq jm$ for some $i,j=1,...,n$ represents the relations between the points $(x_i,y_s)$ and $(x_j,y_d)$ where $s,d=1,...,m$. Then $A_{i,j}=Y_m$ if $x_{ij}=0$ and $A_{i,j}=1_{m\times m}$ if $x_{ij}=1$. 
\end{proof}
%We are now to consider products of minimal finite models of spheres. For this reason we firstly compute the inverse of the matrix associated to $S^n$.  
We compute the determinant of the product of minimal finite models of spheres and consequently the determinant of the product of usual spheres.

\begin{cor} Let $S^n$ and $S^m$ be minimal finite models of spheres of dimension $n$ and $m$ respectively. Then $\det (S^n\times S^m)$ is equal to $1$ if $n$ (or $m$) is an odd number and $-3$ otherwise.
\end{cor}
\begin{proof}
We keep the enumeration previously used for $S^m$ (refer to Remark \ref{rem:esferas}). However, we enumerate the points of $S^n$ in a different manner. We begin with the points located on the left side of the Hasse diagram of $S^n$ (see Figure \ref{fig:esferas_dibujo}), starting from the bottom and moving upwards. Subsequently, we continue in the same manner with the points on the right. Given this enumeration, $S^n$ assumes the following form 
$$\begin{pmatrix}
A & B\\
B & A
\end{pmatrix}, $$
where $A$ corresponds to the matrix of a totally ordered set of $n+1$ points and $B=A+I_{n+1\times n+1}$. In $(S^n\times S^m)_M$, we are changing the entries of $S^n_M$ that are $0$ by $S^{m}_M$ and $1$ by $1_{2(m+1)\times 2(m+1)}$. Hence, $\det(S^n\times S^m)=\det(A'+B')\det(A'-B')$, where $A'$ ($B'$) denotes the matrix $A$ ($B$) after changing its entries using the previous rule. It is clear that $\det(A'-B')=[\det(S^{m}-1_{2(m+1)\times 2(m+1)})]^n=(-1)^{2(m+1)(n+1)}=1$. It remains to study $\det(A'+B')$. Notice that $A'+B'$ is given by
$$\begin{pmatrix}
S^m_M+1 & 2S^m_M &\dots & 2S^m_M\\
2 & \ddots & \ddots& \vdots\\
\vdots & \ddots & S^m_M+1 & 2S^m_M \\
2 & \dots & 2& S^m_M+1
\end{pmatrix}. $$
Substracting the last block column from the other block columns, we get 
$$\begin{pmatrix}
1-S^m_M & 0 &\dots & 0 & 2S^m_M\\
2-2S^m_M & 1-S^m_M & \ddots & \vdots & \vdots\\
\vdots & \ddots &\ddots & 0& 2S^m_M\\
2-2S^m_M& \dots & 2-2S^m_M & 1-S^{m}_M& 2S^m_M \\
1-S^{m}_M & \dots & 1-S^m_M & 1-S^{m}_M & S^m_M+1
\end{pmatrix}.  $$
From this, we deduce that $\det(A'+B')=\det(1-S^m_M)^n\det(1+(-1)^{n}S^m_M)=\det(1+(-1)^{n}S^m_M)$. Suppose $n$ is odd. Then $\det(S^n\times S^m)=1$.  We now compute $\det(S^m_M+1)$. Using the matrix determinant lemma $\det(S^m_M+1)=\det(S^m_M)(1+1S^{m^{-1}}_M 1)$. On the other hand, using Remark \ref{rem:esferas}, we get that $1S^{m^{-1}}_M 1$ is equal to zero if $n$ is odd and equal to $2$ if $n$ is even. From this, we get the last case, that is, $\det (S^n\times S^m)=-3$ if $n$ and $m$ are even numbers.
\end{proof}

