    \field{abstract}{%
    The vertebrate small intestine requires an enormous surface area to
  effectively absorb nutrients from food. Morphological adaptations required to
  establish this extensive surface include generation of an extremely long tube
  and convolution of the absorptive surface of the tube into villi and
  microvilli. In this Review, we discuss recent findings regarding the
  morphogenetic and molecular processes required for intestinal tube elongation
  and surface convolution, examine shared and unique aspects of these processes
  in different species, relate these processes to known human maladies that
  compromise absorptive function and highlight important questions for future
  research.%
    }
