    \field{labelnamesource}{author}
    \field{labeltitlesource}{shorttitle}
    \field{abstract}{%
    The dimensions of the small intestinal diffusion barrier interposed between
  luminal nutrients and their membrane receptors were determined from kinetic
  analysis of substrate hydrolysis by integral surface membrane enzymes. The
  calculated equivalent thickness of the unstirred water layer was too large to
  be compatible with the known dimensions of rat intestine. The discrepancy
  could be reconciled by consideration of the mucous coat overlying the
  intestinal surface membrane. Integral surface membrane proteins could not be
  labeled by an iodine-125 probe unless the surface coat was first removed. The
  mucoprotein surface coat appears to constitute an important diffusion barrier
  for nutrients seeking their digestive and transport sites on the outer
  intestinal membrane.%
    }
