 \section*{Introduction}
We recall \cite{BB} that a $k$-dimensional singular holomorphic foliation   on a complex manifold $X$ of dimension $n$ is defined by  an exact sequence 
% \begin{equation}\label{eq:fol}
$$  0 \to \F \longrightarrow  TX \longrightarrow  N_\F \longrightarrow  0  \,, $$ 
% \end{equation}
where $TX$ is the sheaf of holomorphic vector fields on $X$, the \textit{tangent sheaf} $\F$ is an integrable coherent subsheaf of $TX$ of %generic 
rank $k$, and the \textit{normal sheaf} $N_\F$ is coherent and  torsion free, of %generic
rank $n-k;$ this is the codimension of $\F$. The \emph{singular scheme}, denoted by $\Sing(\F)$, is the locus where the normal sheaf is not locally free. This implies that $\Sing(\F)$ has codimension $\ge 2$. Let $\det (N_\F)$ denote the determinant bundle of the normal sheaf. We prove:

\begin{theorem}\label{Th. main}
Let $X$ be a projective manifold of dimension $n$,  and $\F$  a singular holomorphic foliation of dimension $k > 1$ such that the bundle $\det (N_\F)$ is ample. Let the singular set be $\Sing(\F) = Z \cup  Z_-$, where $Z$ has dimension $k-1$ and  $ Z_-$ consists of lower-dimensional components. Then $Z$ is a connected set. 
\end{theorem}

Our result is sharp since all the assumptions are necessary; see Section \ref{Examples-remarks}.


This answers positively the question raised by D. Cerveau in \cite{Cerv} regarding dimension 2 foliations on \(\mathbb{P}^3\), since the determinant bundle of the normal sheaf of every holomorphic foliation on projective spaces is ample. Cerveau's problem was motivated by the known models of codimension one foliations classified in degrees 0, 1, and 2.



We recall that in the 1960s (see, for instance, \cite{Bott1970}) Raoul Bott investigated global obstructions for the integrability of a subbundle of the tangent bundle of a manifold.  Theorem \ref {Th. main} can be regarded as belonging to that frame of thought, as it provides a global obstruction for a singular holomorphic distribution to be integrable. In fact, we know from  \cite[Theorem 9.5 item 2b.]{CCJ} and \cite[Table 1]{GJM} that there are families of holomorphic distributions of dimension two on \( \mathbb{P}^3 \), whose singular set contains two connected components of dimension 1. 

Our proof uses in an essential way the Baum--Bott residues \cite{BB}. The idea is as follows. By Baum--Bott, the Chern number $c_1(N_\F)^{n-k+1}[X]$ has a residue localized at each \textit{connected } component $Z_1, \dots, Z_s$ of the singular set of dimension $k-1$; these residues are elements in $H_{2k-2}(Z_i)$. Then, the ampleness condition yields, after some work,  that the residue at each $Z_i$ equals the total Chern number  $c_1^{n-k+1} (N_\F)\cap [X]$. Hence  $s=1$. 


We give examples showing that the ampleness condition on the determinant of the normal sheaf is necessary for Theorem \ref{Th. main}. This condition is always satisfied for holomorphic distributions in $\P^n$, see for instance Remark \ref{remark:Pn}.



In \cite{CCGL}, the first author, Cerveau, Giraldo, and Lins Neto asked whether the tangent sheaf of a codimension one foliation must split as a sum of line bundles whenever it is locally free. Recently, the third author, Faenzi and Vall\'es in \cite{FJMV} provided a negative answer to this problem by producing examples   of rational foliations on \(\mathbb{P}^3\) 
of even degree \(2k+4,~ k\geq 0\), whose tangent sheaf is a twisted null-correlation bundle, hence non-split; the singular sets of such foliations are singular, non-reduced curves. Furthermore, these foliations admit flat deformations to non-integrable distributions. 

This naturally leads to the following question: \textit{What are the topological obstructions for a rank $2$ reflexive sheaf to be the tangent sheaf of a foliation in 
a projective 3-fold?}

Next, we present an application of our theorem that emphasizes its interpretation as a topological obstruction to integrability, by imposing a topological condition on the tangent sheaf and in terms of the number of isolated singularities of the foliation. Hence, this provides an answer to the question above, providing a topological obstruction to integrability.




\begin{mcor}\label{cor:3-folds}
Let $\F$ be a codimension one foliation on a smooth projective threefold $X$ satisfying $h^1(\mathcal{O}_X)=0$, and let $C$ be the union of all 1-dimensional components of $\Sing(\F)$; assume that $C$ is reduced and that the determinant of normal bundle of $\F$ is ample. If $h^1(TX\otimes\det(N_\F)^*) = h^2(TX\otimes\det(N_\F)^*)= 0$, then $$h^2(\F\otimes\det(N_\F)^*)=  c_3(\F)\cap [X]$$
which corresponds to the number of isolated singularities of 
$\F$ counted with multiplicities. In particular, when $X = \mathbb{P}^3$, the conditions are satisfied and the formula holds.
\end{mcor}

This is also related to another problem posed by Cerveau in \cite[Problem 2.2]{Cerv}, which concerns the description of foliations whose singular set has pure dimension one, a condition equivalent to the tangent sheaf being locally free (see \cite{CCJ}). The result provides a topological and algebro-geometric obstruction for such foliations. 

In particular, a codimension 1 foliation with   locally free tangent sheaf on a smooth 3-dimensional projective manifold, under the hypotheses of Corollary \ref{cor:3-folds},  must satisfy 
\[
h^2(\F \otimes \det(N_{\F})^*) = 0.
\]
This condition reflects a topological obstruction for a locally free sheaf to be the tangent sheaf of a foliation. 


Moreover, codimension 1  foliations on $\mathbb{P}^3$
with  locally free tangent  sheaf force $\Sing(\F)$ to be singular,  unless 
$\F$ is a pencil of planes. 

\begin{mcor}\label{sing:p3}
Let $\F$ be a codimension one foliation on $\mathbb{P}^3$. If the tangent sheaf of $\F$ is locally free, then either $\F$ is a pencil of planes, or  $\Sing(\F)$ is a singular curve.
\end{mcor}

We emphasize that the hypothesis that $\F$ is locally free is essential: recall that the singular scheme of a generic rational foliation of type $(a,b)$ consists of a smooth complete intersection curve of degree $ab$ plus $$(a+b-2)^3+2(a+b-2)^2+ 2(1-ab)(a+b-2)$$ distinct points, see \cite[Section 10.1]{CCJ} and \cite{CSV}  for the higher-dimensional case. The example in \cite[Theorem 7.1]{FJMV}, where the foliation is given by a pencil and the tangent sheaf is a twisted null-correlation, has as  singular set   a singular  curve. 



