\begin{exe} The Hopf manifolds  $\{\bs H\}$ are compact complex $n$-manifolds which are
 quotients of $\C^{n} \setminus \{0\}$ by the cyclic group generated by a holomorphic contraction. Each of these is $\mathscr{C}^{\infty}$  diffeomorphic to $\mathbb{S}^{2n-1}\times \mathbb{S}^1$. Therefore  $H^2(\bs{H})=\bs{0}$ and  every holomorphic line bundle is flat.  These manifolds are not projective and as we show below, one can construct on them holomorphic codimension 1 foliations (with 
 non-ample normal bundle) with non-connected singular set. 

Let $\alpha,\beta \in \C$ be such that $\alpha / \beta \notin \mathbb{R}$ and $p\neq q\in\Z.$ Consider the 1--form 
\[
\omega_{pq}(x) := 
x_2(p x_3 +\alpha x_1^p x_2^q) dx_1 +
 x_1(qx_3 +\beta x_1^{p}x_2^q) dx_2 -x_1x_2dx_3\,.
\]
As the reader may verify, the semi-simple vector field satisfies the equality
\[
\xymatrix{  \displaystyle \bs{S} = -\beta x_1 \frac{\partial}{\partial x_1} + \alpha x_2 \frac{\partial}{\partial x_2} + (q\alpha - p\beta)x_3 \frac{\partial}{\partial x_3},
}
\]
and therefore $ \omega_{pq}(\bs{S})\equiv0\,.$
Furthermore, the vector field $\bs{S}$ is of Poincar\'e hyperbolic type. In particular, the foliation represented by the 1--form $\omega_{pq}$ is generically transverse to the unit sphere
\[
\mathbb{S}^5 = \{ (x_1, x_2, x_3) \mid x_1\overline{x}_1 + x_2\overline{x}_2 + x_3\overline{x}_3 = 1\}\subset \C^3\setminus\{\bs{0}\},
\]
except at the singular set, which consists of the axes of \(\mathbb{C}^3\) intersecting \(\mathbb{S}^5\) in three circles.

Let $\zeta \in \C$  be such that
\[
\{\Re(-\beta \cdot \zeta), \Re(\alpha \cdot \zeta), \Re((q\alpha - p\beta) \cdot \zeta)\} \subset \{z \in \C \mid \Re(z) < 0\}.\]

In this case, the origin $\bs{0} \in \C^3$ is an attractor of the real flow
\[
\xymatrix@R=1mm{ \mathbb{R} \times \C^3  \ar[r]^{\varphi_{\zeta}} & \C^3 \\
(t, \bs{z}) \ar@{|->}[r] & \exp( (t \cdot \zeta)\cdot \bs{S})(\bs{z}),
}
\]
and the quotient space $\bs{H}_{\zeta} = \mathbb{C}^3 \setminus \{\mathbf{0}\} / \varphi_{\zeta}(1, \cdot)$ is a 3--dimensional Hopf manifold.
The universal covering of this manifold is $\pi: \C^3 \setminus \{\bs{0}\} \to \bs{H}_{\zeta}$.

The foliation $\F(\omega_{pq})$ defined on $\C^3 \setminus \{\bs{0}\}$, being invariant under the vector field $\bs{S}$, induces a holomorphic codimension-one foliation with singularities $\mathscr{G}_{pq}(\zeta)$ on the manifold $M_{\zeta}$. The singular set of the foliation $\mathscr{G}_{pq}(\zeta)$ ) consists of three compact Kupka components 
$K_i = \pi(\{x_j = x_k = 0\}) - \{\bs{0}\}$, each one diffeomorphic to a Riemann surface of genus 1 with the following transversal types:

\vspace{0.5cm}

\[
\begin{array}{||c|c||}
\hline
  \text{Component}   & \text{Transversal Type} \\
                     \hline\hline
K_3 & (p x_2 + \alpha x_1^p x_2^q) dx_1 + (q x_2 + \beta x_1^{p+1} x_2^q) dx_2 \\
  \hline
K_2   & (p x_3 + \alpha x_1^p) dx_1 - x_1 dx_3 \\
  \hline
 K_1   & (q x_3 + \beta x_2^q) dx_2 - x_2 dx_3 \\
  \hline\hline
\end{array}
\]

%\vspace{0.5cm}

%Two observations arise from this construction. First, we can deform the Hopf manifold by varying $\zeta \in \C$ to define the diffeomorphism 
%$\varphi_{\zeta}$. This deformation of the complex structure of 
%$\bs{H}_{\zeta}$ induces deformations of the complex structure of the Kupka components. Additionally, we can deform the foliation by altering the parameters $(\alpha, \beta)$ in the foliation $\omega_{pq}$.
%Finally, this construction can be generalized to any foliation in $\C^n$ with an invariant vector field whose linear part lies in the Poincar\'e domain.
\end{exe}

