The final bound we need for Theorem~\ref{MainTheorem} is:  



\begin{prop} \label{lower bound on anng4 prop} $2^{n^2}\preceq \Ann_{G_4}(n)$.
\end{prop}



\begin{proof}
    Suppose $n \in \N$. Let $u=b^{-1}c^{n^2}s$,  $v=b^{-1}s$, and $w = a^{-n^2}d^{n^2}$. 
  We claim that in $G_4$, 
     $$uw \ = \ b^{-1} c^{n^2} s \, a^{-n^2}   d^{n^2}  \ = \ b^{-1} c^{n^2} a^{-n^2}   d^{n^2} s  \ = \ a^{-n^2}  b^{-1} d^{n^2} s \ = \ a^{-n^2}d^{n^2} \, b^{-1}s \ = \ wv,$$ and so $u$ and $v$ are conjugate in $G_4$. 
        The second of these  equalities holds because $s^a = a s a^{-1} =s^2$ and $s^d = d s d^{-1} =s^2$ in $G_4$ imply that for all $k \in \N$ we have $sa^{-k} =  a^{-k} s^{2^k}$ and $d^{k} s =   s^{2^k} d^{k}$, and therefore $s$ commutes with  $a^{-k} d^k$.   The third uses  $[a,b] = a b a^{-1} b^{-1} = c$ and $[a,c]=[b,c]=1$, from which it follows that $a^{-1} b^{-1}  = b^{-1} c a^{-1}$ and then that $a^{-k} b^{-1}  = b^{-1} c^k a^{-k}$ for all $k \in \Z$.  The fourth uses $[b,d]=1$.    

 Also, $|u|_{G_4}\preceq n$  and $$\Area(u  \, (b^{-1}[a^n,b^n]s)^{-1}) \ \leq \  \Area(b^{-1} c^{n^2}  \, [a^n,b^n]^{-1} b) \ \leq  \ n^3,$$ because $c^{n^2}=[a^n,b^n]$ in $G_4$ as a consequence of apply defining relations $[a,b] = c$ and $[a,c]=1$ and $[b,c]=1$ at most $n^3$ times. So, by Lemma \ref{Gluing van Kampen to annular diagram}, it   suffices to show that $2^{n^2-1} \leq \Ann(u,v)$. 

Well, suppose $\Omega$ is any annular diagram  for $u\sim v$.  A $b$-corridor $\beta$ connects the edge labelled by the $b^{-1}$ in the $u$-boundary component to the   $b^{-1}$ in the $v$-boundary component. Let $w$ be the word on $a$, $c$, and $d$ read along one side of that $b$-corridor, so that cutting the diagram along that side gives a van~Kampen diagram $\Delta$ for $uwv^{-1}w^{-1}$---see Figure~\ref{vK diagram concentric d-rings}.  



