\begin{abstract}
We present a Pontryagin-Guided Direct Policy Optimization (PG-DPO) method for
\emph{constrained} dynamic portfolio choice---incorporating consumption and multi-asset
investment---that scales to \emph{thousands} of risky assets. By combining neural-network
controls with Pontryagin's Maximum Principle (PMP), it circumvents the curse of dimensionality
that renders dynamic programming (DP) grids intractable beyond a handful of assets. Unlike
value-based PDE or BSDE approaches, PG-DPO enforces PMP conditions at each gradient step,
naturally accommodating no-short-selling or borrowing constraints and optional consumption
bounds. A ``one-shot" variant rapidly computes Pontryagin-optimal controls after a brief warm-up, 
leading to substantially higher accuracy than naive baselines.
On modern GPUs, near-optimal solutions often emerge within just one or two minutes of training.
Numerical experiments confirm that, for up to 1{,}000 assets, PG-DPO accurately recovers the known closed-form solution in the unconstrained case and remains tractable under constraints---far exceeding the longstanding DP-based limit of around seven assets.
\end{abstract}











% \begin{keyword} Merton's Portfolio Problem; Pontryagin's Maximum Principle; Direct Policy Optimization; Stochastic Control; Neural Networks \end{keyword}

\section{Introduction}\label{sec:intro}

Dynamic portfolio choice---how an investor should optimally allocate wealth among multiple
assets and consume over time under uncertainty---is a cornerstone of modern finance. Classic
studies by \citet{samuelson1975lifetime} and
\citet{merton1969lifetime,merton1971optimum} pioneered both continuous-time and
discrete-time frameworks in which investors balance growth, risk, and consumption. Under
certain idealized assumptions (e.g., complete markets, unlimited short-selling/borrowing,
and frictionless trade), this ``Merton problem'' admits elegant (semi-)closed-form solutions
\citep[e.g.,][]{kim1996dynamic,liu2007portfolio}. However, real-world markets exhibit
numerous \emph{constraints}---such as short-sale bans, borrowing limits, minimum consumption
requirements, or ratcheting rules that prevent consumption from dropping below past
peaks---which invalidate the unconstrained, frictionless assumption. These practical
features significantly complicate the analysis and typically necessitate more flexible
numerical or simulation-based techniques.


Over the last several decades, a rich literature has emerged that generalizes the Merton
framework to incorporate \emph{policy constraints}. For instance, prohibitions on short
sales or leverage can be encoded through nonnegative constraints on asset holdings,
leading to boundary conditions in the Hamilton--Jacobi--Bellman (HJB) equation or to
variational inequalities in cases where the optimal policy ``touches'' the boundary
\citep[e.g.,][]{karatzas1987optimal, cvitanic1996hedging}.
Borrowing constraints similarly restrict leverage and can require explicit state-space
truncation \citep{karatzas1998methods}. On the consumption side, real-life policies or
economic frictions may impose either lower bounds (to cover essential expenses) or upper
limits, leading to quasi-variational inequalities when multiple constraints interact
\citep{shreve1994optimal, fleming2006controlled}. Additionally, \emph{consumption ratcheting}
\citep{dybvig1995dusenberry} disallows consumption from falling below a previously attained level,
capturing behavioral or habit-formation effects \citep{constantinides1990habit, sundaresan1989intertemporally}.
In all these situations, the classical closed-form Merton solution ceases to apply,
and one must resort to more general numerical schemes.

Even without constraints, dynamic programming (DP) methods for multi-asset Merton problems
tend to suffer from the curse of dimensionality as the number of assets grows. For example,
\citet{campbell1999consumption,campbell2001should,campbell2003multivariate} incorporate
multiple state variables (such as dividend yields and interest rates) but limit the number
of risky assets to fewer than seven for tractability. Empirical studies by
\citet{balduzzi1999transaction}, \citet{lynch2000predictability,lynch2001portfolio}, and
\citet{brandt2005simulation} add learning or transaction costs, yet still consider only
small portfolios. More advanced techniques
\citep{buraschi2010correlation,garlappi2010solving,jurek2011optimal} cannot fully evade
the exponential blow-up in high-dimensional DP grids. Introducing constraints (e.g.,
short-selling bans, consumption floors) on top of many risky assets further amplifies
computational complexity. Consequently, constrained Merton problems in moderate or large
dimensions remain a significant challenge, pushing researchers to explore alternative
approaches.


In this work, we develop a scalable framework that accommodates \emph{thousands} of assets
while naturally handling constraints such as no short-selling or borrowing. Building on
Pontryagin's Maximum Principle (PMP), we propose a \emph{policy-centric} approach---rather
than the usual value-based method---to directly learn optimal controls via neural networks
(Sections~\ref{sec:PMP_multiasset}--\ref{sec:BPTT_multiasset}). Our method avoids
intractable DP grids and instead uses stochastic-gradient updates guided by the Pontryagin
adjoint (costate) processes. The result is a \emph{Pontryagin-Guided Direct Policy
Optimization (PG-DPO)} algorithm that can flexibly incorporate log-barrier or KKT-based
mechanisms to enforce short-sale or borrowing constraints, as well as possible upper/lower
consumption bounds. 

Moreover, we develop a \emph{PG-DPO-OneShot} variant that uses a brief network warm-up,
then \emph{directly} computes the Pontryagin-optimal controls (e.g., via a small-scale
Newton solve under log-barriers) at each time--state, bypassing further network inference
while still respecting all constraints. This approach often yields accuracy improvements 
compared to naive baselines and remains tractable even at
extreme scales (e.g., thousands of assets on a single GPU).


Recent years have seen growing interest in deep learning methods for continuous-time
stochastic control, including deep BSDE techniques \citep{han2018solving,e2017deep}, physics-informed
neural networks \citep{raissi2019physics}, and reinforcement learning \citep{dai2023learning}.
However, most of these approaches rely on approximating \emph{value functions} or PDE
solutions, which become prohibitively difficult to handle with large state/action spaces or
with intricate constraints like short-sale bans and consumption ratcheting. By contrast, our
direct policy optimization method circumvents the explicit value-function approximation,
facilitating scalability to tens of thousands of assets and straightforward enforcement of
portfolios and consumption constraints. Indeed, the policy merely outputs feasible controls
(e.g., via softmax or barrier-based projection), while the Pontryagin adjoint ensures
that gradient updates align with optimality conditions in a forward--backward SDE sense.

\medskip

Our contributions can be summarized as follows:
\begin{enumerate}
\item We formulate a large-scale, continuous-time portfolio--consumption problem under
      realistic constraints (e.g., no short-selling or borrowing, optional consumption bounds),
      highlighting how the classical Merton approach breaks down in such scenarios
      (Section~\ref{sec:Merton_multiasset}).
\item We develop the \textbf{PG-DPO} framework, which parameterizes both investment and
      consumption controls via neural networks, and show how \emph{Pontryagin's Maximum
      Principle} can guide the training even in the presence of constraints
      (Section~\ref{sec:PMP_multiasset}).
\item We propose a \textbf{PG-DPO-OneShot} scheme that leverages a brief warm-up to stabilize
      the Pontryagin adjoint, then computes closed-form or barrier-based solutions for the
      constrained controls at each time step, boosting accuracy and scalability
      (Section~\ref{sec:BPTT_multiasset}).
\item Through numerical experiments (Section~\ref{sec:num_test}), we demonstrate that our
      approach scales to thousands of assets, delivers near-optimal solutions, and enforces
      constraints effectively without resorting to high-dimensional PDE grids.
\end{enumerate}

We conclude in Section~\ref{sec:conclusion} with a discussion of potential extensions, such
as integrating transaction costs, consumption ratcheting, or robust control ideas. Overall,
this work shows that truly large-scale, \emph{constrained} dynamic portfolio choice in a
continuous-time setting---an enduring challenge in mathematical finance---can be made
tractable with Pontryagin-guided, neural-network-based approaches.











\section{Multi-Asset Continuous-Time Portfolio Problem}
\label{sec:Merton_multiasset}

In this section, we present a multi-asset version of Merton's classic portfolio
problem \citep{merton1971optimum}, wherein an investor allocates wealth among multiple risky assets and a
risk-free asset while also consuming continuously. Unlike the single-asset case,
the presence of multiple (potentially correlated) assets with distinct drifts and
volatilities leads to richer portfolio strategies and more challenging solution
methods. In particular, although the \emph{wealth} itself remains one-dimensional,
the \emph{investment decision} can span multiple dimensions, reflecting the
investor's choices across different risky assets.


\subsection{Problem Formulation and Neural Network Parametrization of Controls}
\label{sec:market_model_zero_indexed}

We consider \(n+1\) assets, indexed by \(i=0,1,\dots,n\). The 0th asset is risk-free with drift \(r_t\) and zero volatility, while the remaining \(n\) assets are risky with drifts \(\mu_{i,t}\) for \(i=1,\dots,n\) and covariance \(\Sigma_t \in \mathbb{R}^{n\times n}\). The \(n\)-dimensional drift vector for the risky assets is defined as \(\boldsymbol{\mu}_t = \bigl(\mu_{1,t},\,\dots,\,\mu_{n,t}\bigr)^\top \in \mathbb{R}^{n}\). By setting \(\mu_{0,t} := r_t\) as the drift of the risk-free asset, the \((n+1)\)-dimensional vector encompassing all assets' drifts is given by \(\widetilde{\boldsymbol{\mu}}_t = \bigl(r_t,\;\mu_{1,t},\dots,\mu_{n,t}\bigr)^\top \in \mathbb{R}^{\,n+1}\).

Next, we define a matrix \(\widetilde{V}_t \in \mathbb{R}^{(n+1)\times n}\) such that its 0th row is all zeros, reflecting the zero volatility of the risk-free asset, and the submatrix \(V_t\) governs the \(n\) risky assets. Specifically, \(\widetilde{V}_{t,0,*} = \mathbf{0}\) and \(\widetilde{V}_{t,\,1:n,*} = V_t\), where \(\Sigma_t = V_t\,V_t^\top\).

Let \(X_t\) denote the total wealth, and let \(\boldsymbol{\pi}_t = \bigl(\pi_{0,t},\,\pi_{1,t},\,\dots,\,\pi_{n,t}\bigr)^\top\) represent the portfolio weights satisfying \(\sum_{i=0}^n \pi_{i,t} = 1\). Here, \(\pi_{0,t}\) is the fraction invested in the risk-free asset, and \(\pi_{i,t}\) for \(i \geq 1\) is the fraction invested in the \(i\)th risky asset. The investor's consumption rate \(C_t\) is nonnegative, i.e., \(C_t \ge 0\). The wealth \(X_t\) evolves according to the stochastic differential equation (SDE):
\begin{equation}
dX_t = \Bigl(X_t\,\boldsymbol{\pi}_t^\top\,\widetilde{\boldsymbol{\mu}}_t - C_t\Bigr)\,dt + X_t\,\boldsymbol{\pi}_t^\top\,\widetilde{V}_t\,d\mathbf{W}_t, \quad X_0 = x_0 > 0,
\label{eq:FSDE}
\end{equation}
where \(\mathbf{W}_t \in \mathbb{R}^n\) is an \(n\)-dimensional standard Brownian motion. Note that \(\widetilde{\boldsymbol{\mu}}_{t,0} = r_t\) and \(\widetilde{V}_{t,0,*} = \mathbf{0}\), ensuring that the risk-free component has drift \(r_t\) with no stochastic part.

The utility maximization objective is formulated in continuous time, analogous to the classical Merton problem:
\begin{equation}
J\bigl(\boldsymbol{\pi}_t,\,C_t\bigr) = \mathbb{E}\!\Bigl[\int_{0}^{T} e^{-\rho t}\,U\bigl(C_t\bigr)\,dt + \kappa\,e^{-\rho T}\,U\bigl(X_T\bigr)\Bigr],
\label{eq:objective}
\end{equation}
where \(\rho > 0\) is a continuous discount rate, \(\kappa > 0\) is a bequest parameter, and \(U\) is a CRRA utility function defined as:
\begin{equation}
U(x) = \begin{cases}
\displaystyle
\frac{x^{1-\gamma}}{\,1 - \gamma\,}, & \gamma > 0,\; \gamma \neq 1, \\
\ln(x), & \gamma = 1,
\end{cases}
\label{eq:utility}
\end{equation}
where \(\gamma\) represents the investor's relative risk aversion: higher \(\gamma > 1\) corresponds to greater risk aversion, while \(\gamma < 1\) indicates lower aversion. The logarithmic case (\(\gamma = 1\)) is recovered continuously as \(\gamma \to 1\).

To solve this high-dimensional control problem, the controls are typically parameterized using neural networks:
\begin{equation}
\boldsymbol{\pi}_t = \boldsymbol{\pi}_\theta\bigl(t,\,X_t\bigr) \in \mathbb{R}^n, \quad C_t = C_\phi\bigl(t,\,X_t\bigr),
\label{eq:networks}
\end{equation}
where \(\theta\) and \(\phi\) denote the neural network parameters. The training objective is then \(J(\theta,\phi) \approx J\bigl(\boldsymbol{\pi}_t,\,C_t\bigr)\), and numerical methods are applied to identify a locally or globally optimal policy.






\subsection{Constraints on Portfolio Choice and Consumption}
\label{sec:constraint_multiasset}

In many real-world portfolio settings, investors face constraints that limit the admissible set of controls \(\bigl(\boldsymbol{\pi}_t,\,C_t\bigr)\). Below, we outline two main classes of constraints: nonnegative portfolio weights, which encompass no short-selling and no borrowing, and bounds on consumption.

First, we consider nonnegative portfolio weights. Each weight \(\pi_{i,t}\) is required to satisfy
\[
\pi_{i,t} \ge 0 \quad \text{for} \quad i = 0,1,\dots,n.
\]
Here, \(\pi_{0,t}\) represents the fraction of wealth in the risk-free asset, and \(\pi_{i,t}\) for \(i=1,\dots,n\) are the fractions in risky assets. Because these fractions must be nonnegative, we naturally exclude both short-selling, which would require \(\pi_{i,t} < 0\) for some risky asset \(i\), and borrowing beyond wealth, i.e., a negative position in the risk-free asset \((\pi_{0,t} < 0)\) to leverage the risky assets. Combined with the full-investment condition \(\sum_{i=0}^n \pi_{i,t} = 1\), these nonnegativity constraints ensure a simple, long-only portfolio with no leverage.

Second, consumption rates can also be subject to practical or regulatory requirements. For example, the consumption rate \(C_t\) must satisfy
\[
C_{\min} \le C_t \le C_{\max},
\]
where \(C_{\min} > 0\) might capture mandatory living expenses, and \(C_{\max} < \infty\) might limit overconsumption. More generally, one could allow \(C_{\min}\) or \(C_{\max}\) to be functions of time, such as to reflect seasonal patterns, or of the market path \(S_{<t}\), such as adaptively tightening consumption bounds during downturns.

Beyond nonnegative weights and consumption bounds, many other constraints and market frictions can be incorporated. These include transaction costs or liquidity limits, which effectively modify the wealth dynamics to penalize excessive rebalancing, and taxes or regulatory constraints, such as maximum capital-gain distributions. While these issues are not our primary focus, the Pontryagin-based framework (Sections \ref{sec:PMP_multiasset} and \ref{sec:BPTT_multiasset}) could, in principle, be extended to such settings by restricting the control set or adjusting the state equations. However, such extensions may require methodological adjustments, which lie outside the scope of this paper.

In summary, we focus on a scenario where nonnegative portfolio weights \(\pi_{i,t} \ge 0\) and a full-investment constraint \(\sum_{i=0}^n \pi_{i,t} = 1\) jointly preclude both short-selling and borrowing. In the following sections, we demonstrate how our framework can handle these constraints numerically even when the number of assets is large. While we primarily focus on nonnegative portfolio weights, the consumption constraints can be addressed using similar techniques. Furthermore, if one wishes to allow leverage or certain short positions, it suffices to relax these bounds, such as \(\pi_{0,t} \ge -\alpha\) or \(\pi_{i,t} \ge -\beta\) for some \(\alpha,\beta > 0\), or incorporate more general time-varying limits \([a_i(t), b_i(t)]\). The same methodology applies, under suitable modifications to the admissible set \(\mathcal{A}\).





\subsection{Closed-Form Solutions without Constraints}
\label{sec:closed_form_multiasset}

In some simplified versions of the finite-horizon Merton problem---specifically, unconstrained portfolio choice (no short-sale or leverage limits), CRRA utility, and deterministic parameters \(\boldsymbol{\mu}_t, \Sigma_t, r_t\) (allowing either constant or explicit time dependence)---it is possible to derive closed-form solutions for the portfolio strategy and consumption rate. Below, we outline the core result, keeping in mind that any additional constraints typically invalidate such a closed-form derivation.

If there are no constraints on \(\boldsymbol{\pi}_{1:n,t}\) aside from full investment \(\pi_{0,t} + \sum_{i=1}^n \pi_{i,t} = 1\), and \((\boldsymbol{\mu}_t, \Sigma_t, r_t)\) are deterministic in \(t\), one can often solve the associated HJB equation to obtain a time-invariant (or explicitly time-dependent) investment proportion:
\[
\boldsymbol{\pi}_{1:n,t}^* = \frac{1}{\gamma} \Sigma_t^{-1} \bigl(\boldsymbol{\mu}_t - r_t \mathbf{1}\bigr), \quad \pi_{0,t}^* = 1 - \sum_{i=1}^n \pi_{i,t}^*.
\]
In the simplest case where \(\boldsymbol{\mu}_t, \Sigma_t, r_t\) are truly constant (i.e., \(\mu, \Sigma, r\) do not vary with \(t\)), the above fraction \(\boldsymbol{\pi}_{1:n,t}^*\) is indeed constant in time. If, however, \(\mu_t, \Sigma_t, r_t\) vary deterministically with \(t\), the HJB solution may induce an explicit time dependence in \(\boldsymbol{\pi}_{1:n,t}^*\). Nevertheless, closed-form or semi-analytic formulas are still available in many cases for specific functional forms of \(\mu_t\) and \(\Sigma_t\).

By contrast, the optimal consumption rate \(C_t^*\) in a finite horizon \([0, T]\) generally takes the form
\[
C_t^* = \alpha(t) X_t,
\]
where \(\alpha(t)\) is a time-dependent function determined via a backward solution of the HJB equation. For instance, if \(\mu_t, \Sigma_t, r_t\) are all constant in \(t\), a well-known closed-form result is
\[
\alpha(t) = \frac{\kappa}{\gamma} \left(1 - e^{-\kappa (T - t)}\right)^{-1},
\]
where the decay rate \(\kappa\) is given by
\[
\kappa = \rho - (1 - \gamma) \biggl(r + \frac{(\boldsymbol{\mu} - r\mathbf{1})^\top \Sigma^{-1} (\boldsymbol{\mu} - r\mathbf{1})}{2\gamma}\biggr).
\]
Hence, the consumption fraction \(\alpha(t)\) explicitly depends on the remaining horizon \(T - t\), decaying exponentially as \(t \to T\).

Crucially, these closed-form expressions assume there are no constraints such as short-sale or leverage limits, margin requirements, or path-dependent restrictions. In practice, once even a basic constraint is introduced, the HJB-based analytical solution typically breaks down, necessitating numerical methods. Our proposed Pontryagin-Guided Direct Policy Optimization (Section~\ref{sec:BPTT_multiasset}) is one such scalable approach, capable of handling high-dimensional or constraint-rich problems beyond the reach of closed-form Merton formulas.












\section{Pontryagin's Principle for Multi-Asset Portfolio Problem}
\label{sec:PMP_multiasset}

We now discuss how Pontryagin's Maximum Principle (PMP) applies to the
\emph{multi-asset, continuous-time} Merton problem introduced in
Section~\ref{sec:market_model_zero_indexed}. Although the \emph{wealth} \(X_t\) itself is a
single-dimensional state, the presence of multiple risky assets can introduce
higher-dimensional controls \(\boldsymbol{\pi}_t\in\mathbb{R}^n\). 

In particular, we first derive the adjoint (costate) processes for the unconstrained case, where no restrictions are imposed on the portfolio weights \(\boldsymbol{\pi}_t\). We then extend this analysis to the constrained case, where nonnegative portfolio weights \(\pi_{i,t} \ge 0\) and the full-investment condition \(\sum_{i=0}^n \pi_{i,t} = 1\) are enforced, as discussed in Section~\ref{sec:constraint_multiasset}. In both scenarios, we explore the relationship between the adjoint processes and the optimal policies, as well as their implications for suboptimal (parameterized) policies in this multi-asset setting.



\subsection{Pontryagin's Maximum Principle for the Continuous-Time Portfolio Problem}
\label{sec:PMP_multiasset_zero_index}

We now apply Pontryagin's Maximum Principle to the continuous-time portfolio model outlined in Section~\ref{sec:market_model_zero_indexed}. See
\citealp{pontryagin1962the,pardouxpeng1990adapted,fleming2006controlled,pham2009continuous}
for further details on PMP. Following Pontryagin's Maximum Principle, we introduce a scalar adjoint \(\lambda_t \approx \tfrac{\partial J}{\partial X_t}\), tied to the state \(X_t\) in the utility maximization objective \(J\) defined in \eqref{eq:objective}, and a vector adjoint \(\mathbf{Z}_t \in \mathbb{R}^n\), tied to the \(n\)-dimensional Brownian motion \(\mathbf{W}_t\).

The Hamiltonian then takes the form
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{H}\!\Bigl(t,\,X_t,\,\boldsymbol{\pi}_t,\,C_t,\,\lambda_t,\,\mathbf{Z}_t\Bigr) 
\;=\; e^{-\rho t}\,U\bigl(C_t\bigr) 
\;+\; \lambda_t\,\Bigl[X_t\,\boldsymbol{\pi}_t^\top\,\widetilde{\mu}_t - C_t\Bigr] 
\;+\; \mathbf{Z}_t^\top\bigl(X_t\,\widetilde{V}_t^\top\,\boldsymbol{\pi}_t\bigr),
\label{eq:Hamiltonian}
\end{equation}
where \(\lambda_t\) captures the marginal value of \(X_t\), and \(\mathbf{Z}_t\) captures sensitivities to the Brownian shocks \(\mathbf{W}_t\).

When the controls \(\bigl(\boldsymbol{\pi}_t^*,\,C_t^*\bigr)\) are optimal, the wealth process \(X_t^*\) and adjoint processes \(\bigl(\lambda_t^*,\,\mathbf{Z}_t^*\bigr)\) jointly satisfy the \emph{coupled forward--backward Pontryagin system}:
\begin{align}
dX_t^* \;&=\; \Bigl[X_t^*\,\bigl(\boldsymbol{\pi}_t^*\bigr)^\top \widetilde{\mu}_t 
  \;-\; C_t^*\Bigr]\,dt 
  \;+\; X_t^*\bigl(\boldsymbol{\pi}_t^*\bigr)^\top \widetilde{V}_t\, d\mathbf{W}_t, 
  \quad X_0^*=x_0>0, 
\label{eq:FBSDE}
\\
d\lambda_t^* 
\;&=\; -\,\frac{\partial\mathcal{H}}{\partial X} 
  \Bigl(t,\,X_t^*,\,\boldsymbol{\pi}_t^*,\,C_t^*,\,\lambda_t^*,\,\mathbf{Z}_t^*\Bigr)\,dt 
  \;+\; \mathbf{Z}_t^{*\top}\,d\mathbf{W}_t, 
  \quad \lambda_T^* \;=\; 
  \frac{\partial}{\partial X}\Bigl[\kappa\,e^{-\rho T}\,U\bigl(X_T^*\bigr)\Bigr].
\nonumber
\end{align}
Here, \(X_t^*\) follows the same structural dynamics as \eqref{eq:FSDE} but with optimal controls \(\boldsymbol{\pi}_t^*\) and \(C_t^*\), while \(\lambda_t^*\) and \(\mathbf{Z}_t^*\) solve the backward adjoint equation. The coupling arises because \(\boldsymbol{\pi}_t^*\) and \(C_t^*\) depend on \(\lambda_t^*\), which in turn depends on \(X_t^*\) through \(\partial\mathcal{H}/\partial X\). This closed-loop structure distinguishes optimal trajectories from generic solutions to \eqref{eq:FSDE}.

Additionally, at each \(t\), \(\bigl(\boldsymbol{\pi}_t^*,\,C_t^*\bigr)\) maximizes the Hamiltonian \(\mathcal{H}\) locally, yielding the first-order conditions
\[
\frac{\partial \mathcal{H}}{\partial C_t} 
\;=\; e^{-\rho t}\,U'\bigl(C_t^*\bigr) - \lambda_t^* = 0,
\quad
\frac{\partial \mathcal{H}}{\partial \boldsymbol{\pi}_t} 
\;=\; \lambda_t^*\,X_t^*\,\widetilde{\mu}_t 
  \;+\; X_t^*\,\widetilde{V}_t^\top\,\mathbf{Z}_t^* = \mathbf{0}.
\]
Hence, one obtains
\[
e^{-\rho t}\,U'\bigl(C_t^*\bigr) = \lambda_t^*,
\quad
\widetilde{V}_t^\top\,\mathbf{Z}_t^* 
= -\,\lambda_t^*\;\widetilde{\mu}_t.
\]
Given the utility function \(U\) in \eqref{eq:utility}, its marginal is \(U'(c) = c^{-\gamma}\). Substituting into \(e^{-\rho t}\,U'\bigl(C_t^*\bigr) = \lambda_t^*\) directly yields:
\begin{equation}
C_t^* = \Bigl(e^{\rho t}\,\lambda_t^*\Bigr)^{-\tfrac{1}{\gamma}}.
\label{eq:C_opt_unconstrained}
\end{equation}
Thus, the optimal consumption rate depends on \(\lambda_t^*\) but does not explicitly require its full functional form, thanks to the power-law structure.

A common result from the backward SDE for \(\lambda_t^*\) (or by differentiating \(\mathcal{H}\) w.r.t.\ \(X_t\)) is
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{Z}_t^* 
\;=\; \bigl(\partial_x \lambda_t^*\bigr) 
  \,\Bigl(X_t^*\,\widetilde{V}_t^\top\,\boldsymbol{\pi}_t^*\Bigr),
\label{eq:Z_from_pi}
\end{equation}
which says that \(\mathbf{Z}_t^*\) equals the product of \(\bigl(X_t^*\,\widetilde{V}_t^\top\,\boldsymbol{\pi}_t^*\bigr)\) and the scalar \(\partial_x \lambda_t^*\). For the sub-vector \(\mathbf{Z}_{1:n,t}^*\in\mathbb{R}^n\) of \(\mathbf{Z}_t^*\) (the part tied to the \(n\) risky Brownian directions), one often has
\[
\mathbf{V}_t^\top\,\mathbf{Z}_{1:n,t}^* 
\;=\; -\,\lambda_t^*\,\bigl(\mu_{1,t}-r_t,\;\dots,\;\mu_{n,t}-r_t\bigr)^\top,
\]
while from
\[
\mathbf{Z}_{1:n,t}^* 
\;=\; \bigl(\partial_x \lambda_t^*\bigr)\; X_t^*\;\mathbf{V}_t^\top\,
       \bigl(\boldsymbol{\pi}_{1:n,t}^*\bigr),
\]
one obtains
\[
\mathbf{V}_t^\top\Bigl[\bigl(\partial_x \lambda_t^*\bigr)\, X_t^*\,
  \mathbf{V}_t^\top\,\boldsymbol{\pi}_{1:n,t}^*\Bigr] 
\;=\; -\,\lambda_t^*\;\bigl(\mu_{1,t}-r_t,\;\dots,\;\mu_{n,t}-r_t\bigr)^\top.
\]
Solving for \(\boldsymbol{\pi}_{1:n,t}^*\) yields
\begin{equation}
\boldsymbol{\pi}_{1:n,t}^* 
\;=\; -\,\frac{\lambda_t^*}{
  \,X_t^*\,\bigl(\partial_x \lambda_t^*\bigr)\,} \;\Sigma_t^{-1}\! 
  \bigl(\mu_{1,t}-r_t,\;\dots,\;\mu_{n,t}-r_t\bigr),
\label{eq:pi_opt_unconstrained}
\end{equation}
recovering the familiar \(\Sigma_t^{-1}\bigl(\boldsymbol{\mu}_t - r_t\,\mathbf{1}\bigr)\) shape in multi-asset Merton problems.

Overall, Pontryagin's approach reduces the continuous-time control problem to a forward--backward SDE (for \(X_t^*,\lambda_t^*,\mathbf{Z}_t^*\)) plus a pointwise maximization yielding closed-form formulas for \(\bigl(\boldsymbol{\pi}_t^*,\,C_t^*\bigr)\). In modern ML frameworks (e.g., \texttt{PyTorch}), one can compute these adjoints automatically by backpropagating through stochastic rollouts of \(X_t\), thus avoiding PDE or grid-based methods. This principle underlies the neural Pontryagin-based algorithms introduced later.








\subsection{Extending Pontryagin's Maximum Principle to Constrained Portfolio Problems}
\label{sec:PMP_constrained}

\subsubsection{KKT-Based Approach to Constrained Portfolio Problems}
\label{sec:KKT_short_nosell_sum1}

Many realistic portfolio settings impose constraints on the portfolio weights \(\boldsymbol{\pi}_t\)
or on consumption \(C_t\). In this subsection, we illustrate how to incorporate such constraints
using a KKT (Karush--Kuhn--Tucker) formulation 
\citep[see][for foundational references]{karush1939minima,kuhn2013nonlinear}
and further discussion in standard texts 
\citep[e.g.][]{nocedal1999numerical}.
We focus on the case \(\pi_{i,t} \ge 0\) (i.e., no short-selling or borrowing) 
together with \(\sum_{i=0}^n \pi_{i,t} = 1\), which signifies full investment in the risk-free plus \(n\) risky assets. 
Here, \(\pi_{0,t}\) represents the proportion in the risk-free asset, while \(\pi_{i,t}\) for \(i \ge 1\) 
are the weights in the risky assets. Although we concentrate on these two constraints, 
the framework naturally extends to others discussed in Section~\ref{sec:constraint_multiasset}.

We express these constraints via equality and inequality functions:
\[
h\bigl(\boldsymbol{\pi}_t\bigr) = 1 - \sum_{i=0}^n \pi_{i,t} = 0,
\qquad
g_i\bigl(\boldsymbol{\pi}_t\bigr) = -\,\pi_{i,t} \le 0,
\quad i = 0, \dots, n.
\]
Next, we introduce the Lagrange multiplier \(\eta_t\) for the equality constraint 
and multipliers \(\zeta_{i,t} \ge 0\) for the inequalities.
We then build an augmented Hamiltonian:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:Hamiltonian_kkt}
\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{\mathrm{KKT}} 
\;=\; 
\mathcal{H}
\;+\;
\eta_t\,h\bigl(\boldsymbol{\pi}_t\bigr)
\;+\;
\sum_{i=0}^n \zeta_{i,t}\,g_i\bigl(\boldsymbol{\pi}_t\bigr),
\end{equation}
where \(\mathcal{H}\) is the standard Pontryagin Hamiltonian 
\(\mathcal{H}(t, X_t, \boldsymbol{\pi}_t, C_t, \lambda_t, \mathbf{Z}_t)\). 
By KKT theory, the first-order optimality conditions at each time \(t\) become
\[
\frac{\partial \widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{\mathrm{KKT}}}{\partial \boldsymbol{\pi}_t} 
= \mathbf{0},
\quad 
h\bigl(\boldsymbol{\pi}_t\bigr) = 0,
\quad 
g_i\bigl(\boldsymbol{\pi}_t\bigr)\le 0,\;\zeta_{i,t}\ge 0,\;\zeta_{i,t}\,g_i\bigl(\boldsymbol{\pi}_t\bigr) = 0.
\]
The partial derivative \(\tfrac{\partial \widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{\mathrm{KKT}}}{\partial \pi_{i,t}}\) 
incorporates both \(\eta_t\) and \(\zeta_{i,t}\). Concretely,
\[
\frac{\partial \widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{\mathrm{KKT}}}{\partial \pi_{i,t}}
= 
\frac{\partial \mathcal{H}}{\partial \pi_{i,t}} 
+ 
\eta_t \,\frac{\partial h}{\partial \pi_{i,t}}
+ 
\zeta_{i,t}\,\frac{\partial g_i}{\partial \pi_{i,t}}
\;=\;
0,
\quad 
i = 0,\dots,n,
\]
with \(\tfrac{\partial h}{\partial \pi_{i,t}} = -1\) and 
\(\tfrac{\partial g_i}{\partial \pi_{i,t}} = -1\) if \(g_i=-\pi_{i,t}\).  
Since each \(\zeta_{i,t} \ge 0\), we obtain a piecewise system:
\[
\sum_{i=0}^n \pi_{i,t} = 1,
\quad
\zeta_{i,t}\,\pi_{i,t} = 0
\quad
(\text{complementary slackness}).
\]
Hence, if \(\pi_{i,t} > 0\), then the multiplier \(\zeta_{i,t}\) is zero (the inequality is inactive). 
Conversely, if \(\pi_{i,t} = 0\), then \(\zeta_{i,t} \ge 0\) may be strictly positive, enforcing the boundary.

If we \emph{ignore} the constraints, the unconstrained Pontryagin solution for \(\boldsymbol{\pi}_t\) 
might yield negative entries or fail to sum to one. The KKT system effectively \emph{projects} 
this unconstrained solution onto the feasible simplex
\[
\Delta^n
=\;
\Bigl\{\boldsymbol{\pi}_t \in \mathbb{R}^{n+1} \,\big|\,
\sum_{i=0}^n \pi_{i,t}=1,\;\pi_{i,t}\ge0
\Bigr\}.
\]
Numerically, this often requires an iterative active-set method to determine which assets 
are pinned at zero and which remain positive while ensuring \(\sum_{i=0}^n \pi_{i,t}=1\). 
In short, the KKT conditions 
\citep[cf.\ e.g.][]{karush1939minima,kuhn2013nonlinear,nocedal1999numerical} 
act as a rigorous framework for enforcing no short-selling, no borrowing, and full investment 
within the Pontryagin approach.







\subsubsection{Barrier-Based Approach to Constrained Portfolio Problems}
\label{sec:barrier_sum1_nosell_Zsub_sigma}

The KKT approach provides a rigorous way to handle short-selling or borrowing constraints but becomes prohibitively expensive in high-dimensional settings due to the need to repeatedly solve large, piecewise nonlinear systems. An alternative is the \emph{log-barrier} method \citep[e.g.,][]{fiacco1990nonlinear,boyd2004convex,nocedal1999numerical}, which enforces \(\pi_{i,t} > 0\) by introducing a penalty term:
\[
\Phi_{\epsilon}\bigl(\pi_{i,t}\bigr)
\;=\;
-\,\epsilon \,\ln\bigl(\pi_{i,t}\bigr),
\quad \epsilon > 0.
\]
As \(\pi_{i,t} \to 0^+\), \(\Phi_{\epsilon}(\pi_{i,t})\) diverges to \(-\infty\), effectively preventing \(\pi_{i,t}\) from approaching zero. This method ensures \(\pi_{i,t}>0\) smoothly and approximately, avoiding explicit boundary detection. As \(\epsilon\to0\), the solution converges to the unconstrained case, making the barrier approach practical for large-scale portfolio optimization.

To handle the constraints \(\sum_{i=0}^n \pi_{i,t} = 1\) and \(\pi_{i,t} > 0\), we augment the original Pontryagin Hamiltonian \(\mathcal{H}\) with a Lagrange multiplier \(\eta_t\) for the equality constraint and log-barrier terms \(\Phi_{\epsilon}(\pi_{i,t})\) for the positivity constraints. The augmented Hamiltonian is given by:
\begin{equation}
\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{\mathrm{barrier}}
\;=\;
\mathcal{H}
\;+\;
\eta_t \Bigl(1 - \sum_{i=0}^n \pi_{i,t}\Bigr)
\;+\;
\epsilon \sum_{i=0}^n \ln\!\bigl(\pi_{i,t}\bigr).
\label{eq:Hamiltonian_barrier}
\end{equation}
Here, \(\eta_t\) enforces the sum-to-one constraint, while \(\Phi_{\epsilon}\) penalizes small \(\pi_{i,t}\).

From the log-barrier terms, we derive the modified first-order conditions:
\[
\frac{\partial \mathcal{H}}{\partial \pi_{i,t}}
\;=\;
\eta_t
\;+\;
\frac{\epsilon}{\pi_{i,t}},
\quad
i=0,\dots,n,
\quad
\text{with}
\;
\sum_{i=0}^n \pi_{i,t} = 1.
\]
For instance, in a multi-asset Merton model with a risk-free asset \(i=0\) and \(n\) risky assets \(i=1,\dots,n\), we can write:
\[
  \frac{\partial \mathcal{H}}{\partial \pi_{0,t}}
  \;=\;
  \lambda_t^*\,X_t^*\,r_t
  \;=\;
  \eta_t
  \;+\;
  \frac{\epsilon}{\pi_{0,t}},
\]
\[
  \frac{\partial \mathcal{H}}{\partial \pi_{i,t}}
  \;=\;
  \lambda_t^*\,X_t^*\,\mu_{i,t}
  \;+\;
  (\partial_x\lambda_t^*)(X_t^*)^2 \bigl[\Sigma_t\,\boldsymbol{\pi}_{t,1:n}^*\bigr]_i
  \;=\;
  \eta_t
  \;+\;
  \epsilon\,\frac{1}{\pi_{i,t}},
  \quad
  i=1,\dots,n.
\]
Solving these simultaneously, together with \(\sum_{i=0}^n \pi_{i,t}=1\), yields a smooth approximation of the constrained optimum.

To implement the barrier method numerically, we define a function \(\mathbf{F} : \mathbb{R}^{\,n+2} \to \mathbb{R}^{\,n+2}\) as:
\[
\mathbf{F}\bigl(\boldsymbol{\pi}_t,\eta_t\bigr)
\;=\;
\Bigl(F_0,\dots,F_n,\;F_{\mathrm{sum}}\Bigr)^\top,
\]
where each component encodes either a barrier-FOC or the sum-to-one constraint:
\[
F_i(\boldsymbol{\pi}_t,\eta_t)
\;=\;
\frac{\partial \mathcal{H}}{\partial \pi_{i,t}}(\boldsymbol{\pi}_t)
\;-\;
\Bigl(\eta_t + \frac{\epsilon}{\pi_{i,t}}\Bigr),
\quad
i=0,\dots,n,
\]
\[
F_{\mathrm{sum}}(\boldsymbol{\pi}_t,\eta_t)
\;=\;
\sum_{i=0}^n \pi_{i,t} - 1.
\]
Finding \(\mathbf{F}(\boldsymbol{\pi}_t,\eta_t)=\mathbf{0}\) is equivalent to solving:
\[
\begin{cases}
\displaystyle
\frac{\partial \mathcal{H}}{\partial \pi_{i,t}}(\boldsymbol{\pi}_t)
= \eta_t + \frac{\epsilon}{\pi_{i,t}},
& i=0,\dots,n,
\\[6pt]
\displaystyle
\sum_{i=0}^n \pi_{i,t} = 1.
&
\end{cases}
\]
This enforces both the barrier-based first-order conditions (FOCs) and the sum-to-one constraint.

The Jacobian \(D\mathbf{F}(\boldsymbol{\pi}_t,\eta_t)\) is an \((n+2)\times(n+2)\) matrix.
Ordering the unknowns as \(\bigl(\,\pi_{0,t},\dots,\pi_{n,t},\;\eta_t\bigr)\),
the rows of \(D\mathbf{F}\) correspond to \(\bigl(F_0,\dots,F_n,F_{\mathrm{sum}}\bigr)\). Concretely:
\[
  D\mathbf{F}(\boldsymbol{\pi}_t,\eta_t)
  \;=\;
  \begin{pmatrix}
    \tfrac{\partial F_0}{\partial \pi_{0,t}} & \cdots & \tfrac{\partial F_0}{\partial \pi_{n,t}} & \tfrac{\partial F_0}{\partial \eta_t} \\[2pt]
    \vdots & \ddots & & \vdots \\[2pt]
    \tfrac{\partial F_n}{\partial \pi_{0,t}} & \cdots & \tfrac{\partial F_n}{\partial \pi_{n,t}} & \tfrac{\partial F_n}{\partial \eta_t} \\[2pt]
    \tfrac{\partial F_{\mathrm{sum}}}{\partial \pi_{0,t}} & \cdots & \tfrac{\partial F_{\mathrm{sum}}}{\partial \pi_{n,t}} & \tfrac{\partial F_{\mathrm{sum}}}{\partial \eta_t}
  \end{pmatrix}.
\]
Using the definitions of \(F_i\) and \(F_{\mathrm{sum}}\), we deduce each partial derivative.
For \(\tfrac{\partial F_i}{\partial \pi_{j,t}}\):
\[
  \frac{\partial F_i}{\partial \pi_{j,t}}
  \;=\;
  \frac{\partial^2 \mathcal{H}}{\partial \pi_{i,t} \partial \pi_{j,t}}
  \;-\;
  \delta_{ij}\,\frac{\epsilon}{\pi_{i,t}^2},
  \quad
  0 \le i,j \le n,
\]
where \(\tfrac{\partial^2 \mathcal{H}}{\partial \pi_{i,t} \partial \pi_{j,t}}\) is the second derivative of \(\mathcal{H}\) w.r.t.\ \(\pi_{i,t},\pi_{j,t}\), and \(\delta_{ij}\) is the Kronecker delta ensuring that \(-\,\epsilon/\pi_{i,t}^2\) appears only on the diagonal.
When both $i,j \ge 1$ (risky assets), we have
\[
  \frac{\partial^2 \mathcal{H}}{\partial \pi_{i,t}\partial \pi_{j,t}}
  \;=\;
  \bigl(\partial_x \lambda_t^*\bigr)\,(X_t^*)^2\,\Sigma_{ij},
\]
whereas if either $i=0$ or $j=0$ (risk-free asset involved), then
\[
  \frac{\partial^2 \mathcal{H}}{\partial \pi_{i,t}\partial \pi_{j,t}} = 0.
\]
For \(\tfrac{\partial F_i}{\partial \eta_t}\):
\[
  \frac{\partial F_i}{\partial \eta_t}
  \;=\;
  -\,1,
  \quad
  0\le i\le n.
\]
For \(F_{\mathrm{sum}}\):
\[
  \frac{\partial F_{\mathrm{sum}}}{\partial \pi_{j,t}}
  \;=\;
  1,
  \quad
  \frac{\partial F_{\mathrm{sum}}}{\partial \eta_t}
  \;=\;
  0,
  \quad
  0\le j\le n.
\]
Assembling these partial derivatives results in an \((n+2)\times(n+2)\) system. 

Solving \(\mathbf{F}(\boldsymbol{\pi}_t,\eta_t)=\mathbf{0}\) can be done via Newton's method \citep[][Chap.~3]{nocedal1999numerical}:
\[
  D\mathbf{F}(\boldsymbol{\pi}_t^{(k)},\eta_t^{(k)})\,\Delta
  =
  -\mathbf{F}(\boldsymbol{\pi}_t^{(k)},\eta_t^{(k)}\bigr),
  \quad
  \begin{pmatrix}
    \boldsymbol{\pi}_t^{(k+1)} \\[3pt]
    \eta_t^{(k+1)}
  \end{pmatrix}
  =
  \begin{pmatrix}
    \boldsymbol{\pi}_t^{(k)} \\[3pt]
    \eta_t^{(k)}
  \end{pmatrix}
  +
  \alpha_k\,\Delta,
\]
where \(0<\alpha_k\le1\) is chosen (e.g., via a simple backtracking line search) so that \(\pi_{i,t}^{(k+1)}>0\). Repeating this process until \(\|\mathbf{F}(\boldsymbol{\pi}_t^{(k)},\eta_t^{(k)})\|\to0\) yields the barrier-based solution. As \(\epsilon\to0\), it converges to the unconstrained Merton policy, except for any \(\pi_{i,t}\) pinned near zero.

The barrier-based procedure provides a stable, scalable way to enforce \(\pi_{i,t}\ge0\) and \(\sum_{i=0}^n\pi_{i,t}=1\) in large-scale continuous-time portfolio problems. It avoids the heavy cost of KKT-based active-set methods while maintaining positivity and scalability. A moderate \(\epsilon\) balances strict positivity with computational tractability, as very small \(\epsilon\) approximates the exact inequality constraints but risks ill-conditioning. Sparse or iterative solvers can mitigate costs for large \(n\), making this approach practical for high-dimensional settings.











\subsection{Policy-Fixed Adjoint Processes and Parameter Gradients}
\label{sec:suboptimal_policies_multiasset}

In neural network implementations, suboptimal policies \(\bigl(\boldsymbol{\pi}_\theta,C_\phi\bigr)\) can still induce well-defined adjoint processes through an \emph{augmented} Hamiltonian \(\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}\) as in \eqref{eq:Hamiltonian_kkt} and \eqref{eq:Hamiltonian_barrier}. This Hamiltonian subsumes both the standard Pontryagin structure and any constraint mechanisms (e.g., KKT multipliers or barrier terms). Formally, the \emph{policy-fixed adjoint} \(\bigl(\lambda_t,\mathbf{Z}_t\bigr)\) satisfies the backward stochastic differential equation (BSDE):
\begin{align}
d\lambda_t
&=\;
-\;\frac{\partial}{\partial X}\,
\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}\Bigl(
  t,\,X_t,\,\boldsymbol{\pi}_\theta(t,X_t),\,C_\phi(t,X_t),\,\lambda_t,\,\mathbf{Z}_t
\Bigr)\,dt
\;+\;
\mathbf{Z}_t^\top\,d\mathbf{W}_t,
\nonumber\\[3pt]
\lambda_T
&=\;\frac{\partial}{\partial X}
\Bigl[\kappa\,e^{-\rho T}\,U\bigl(X_T\bigr)\Bigr],
\label{eq:adjoint_BSDE_policy_fixed}
\end{align}
where \(X_t\) evolves under the \emph{suboptimal} policy \(\bigl(\boldsymbol{\pi}_\theta,\,C_\phi\bigr)\). This stands in contrast to the \emph{optimal} adjoint processes \(\bigl(\lambda_t^*, \mathbf{Z}_t^*\bigr)\) in \eqref{eq:FBSDE}, which are associated with the true optimal controls \(\bigl(\boldsymbol{\pi}_t^*,\,C_t^*\bigr)\). The suboptimal adjoint \(\bigl(\lambda_t,\mathbf{Z}_t\bigr)\) thus quantifies local sensitivities relative to the parameterized (possibly non-optimal) controls.

When constraints are added (e.g., no short-selling, consumption bounds), we \emph{augment} the original Hamiltonian as
\[
\widetilde{\mathcal{H}} 
\;=\;
\mathcal{H}
\;+\;
\bigl(\!\text{barrier/KKT/etc.\ terms}\bigr).
\]
While these additive terms change the \emph{content} of \(\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}\), they do not alter the structural form of the backward equation \eqref{eq:adjoint_BSDE_policy_fixed}. Mathematically, the adjoint SDE depends only on the first-order derivative \(\partial \widetilde{\mathcal{H}}/\partial X\), which remains well-defined regardless of how constraints are imposed. This unification allows both unconstrained and constrained problems to be handled consistently within a single theoretical framework.

Modern deep learning frameworks (e.g., \texttt{PyTorch}) do \emph{not} numerically solve \eqref{eq:adjoint_BSDE_policy_fixed} directly. Instead, they leverage the fact that the adjoint process \(\lambda_t\) represents the sensitivity of the performance functional \(J\), defined in \eqref{eq:objective}, with respect to the state \(X_t\):
\[
\lambda_t \;=\; \frac{\partial J}{\partial X_t}.
\]
This quantity is computed automatically via backpropagation through the computational graph of \(X_t\). Once \(\lambda_t\) is obtained, the process \(\mathbf{Z}_t\) can be recovered through an additional relation \eqref{eq:Z_from_pi}, which links \(\partial_x \lambda_t\) to \(\mathbf{Z}_t\) via the policy-driven diffusion. As a result, \(\bigl(\lambda_t,\mathbf{Z}_t\bigr)\) emerge as byproducts of the gradient calculation \(\nabla_{\theta,\phi} J\), bypassing the need for a standalone BSDE solver and significantly simplifying the time discretization process.

We now define the \emph{network-based} objective, \(J(\theta,\phi)\), as an approximation of 
\eqref{eq:objective} that employs the neural-network controls 
\(\bigl(\boldsymbol{\pi}_\theta,\,C_\phi\bigr)\).
 Then, the parameter gradients adopt a Pontryagin-like form \citep[see][]{ma1999forward,yong2012stochastic}:
\begin{align}
\nabla_\theta J 
&=\;
\mathbb{E}\!\Bigl[
  \int_0^T
    \Bigl(\lambda_t\,\tfrac{\partial b}{\partial \theta}
          \;+\;
          \mathbf{Z}_t^\top\,\tfrac{\partial \sigma}{\partial \theta}\Bigr)\,dt
\Bigr]
\;+\;
(\text{direct payoff term in }\theta),
\label{eq:grad_theta_symbolic_zeroindexed}
\\[3pt]
\nabla_\phi J
&=\;
\mathbb{E}\!\Bigl[
  \int_0^T
    \Bigl(\lambda_t\,\tfrac{\partial b}{\partial \phi}
          \;+\;
          \mathbf{Z}_t^\top\,\tfrac{\partial \sigma}{\partial \phi}\Bigr)\,dt
\Bigr]
\;+\;
(\text{direct payoff term in }\phi).
\label{eq:grad_phi_symbolic_zeroindexed}
\end{align}
Here, \(\lambda_t\) and \(\mathbf{Z}_t\) are precisely the \emph{policy-fixed} adjoint processes from \eqref{eq:adjoint_BSDE_policy_fixed}, while \(b\) and \(\sigma\) denote the drift and diffusion of \(X_t\) under \(\bigl(\boldsymbol{\pi}_\theta,C_\phi\bigr)\).  

Following Section~\ref{sec:market_model_zero_indexed}, let
\[
b\bigl(t,X_t,\boldsymbol{\pi}_t,C_t\bigr)
=\;
X_t\,\boldsymbol{\pi}_t^\top\,\widetilde{\boldsymbol{\mu}}_t
\;-\;
C_t,
\quad
\sigma\bigl(t,X_t,\boldsymbol{\pi}_t\bigr)
=\;
X_t\,\boldsymbol{\pi}_t^\top\,\widetilde{V}_t,
\]
where $\widetilde{\boldsymbol{\mu}}_t \in \mathbb{R}^{n+1}$ collects the risk-free rate $r_t$ and the $n$ risky drifts $\mu_{i,t}$, while $\widetilde{V}_t\in\mathbb{R}^{(n+1)\times n}$ encodes a zero row for the risk-free asset plus the factorization $V_t$ of $\Sigma_t$. Defining $\boldsymbol{\pi}_t=\boldsymbol{\pi}_\theta(t,X_t)$ and $C_t=C_\phi(t,X_t)$, the partial derivatives become:
\[
\frac{\partial b}{\partial \theta}
=\;
X_t\,\widetilde{\boldsymbol{\mu}}_t^\top
\,\frac{\partial\boldsymbol{\pi}_\theta}{\partial\theta},
\quad
\frac{\partial b}{\partial \phi}
=\;
-\,\frac{\partial C_\phi}{\partial \phi},
\quad
\frac{\partial \sigma}{\partial \theta}
=\;
X_t\,\widetilde{V}_t^\top
\,\frac{\partial\boldsymbol{\pi}_\theta}{\partial\theta},
\quad
\frac{\partial \sigma}{\partial \phi}
=\;
0.
\]
Additionally, the direct utility of consumption 
\(\int_0^T e^{-\rho t}\,U\bigl(C_\phi(t,X_t)\bigr)\,dt\)
contributes
\[
\frac{\partial}{\partial \phi}
\Bigl[
  e^{-\rho t}\,U\bigl(C_\phi(t,X_t)\bigr)
\Bigr]
=\;
e^{-\rho t}\,U'\bigl(C_\phi(\cdot)\bigr)\,
\frac{\partial C_\phi(\cdot)}{\partial \phi}.
\]
Substituting into \eqref{eq:grad_theta_symbolic_zeroindexed}--\eqref{eq:grad_phi_symbolic_zeroindexed} yields the \emph{expanded} expressions:
\begin{align}
\nabla_{\theta}J 
&=\;
\mathbb{E}\!\Bigl[
  \int_0^T
    \Bigl\{
      \lambda_t\,X_t\,\widetilde{\boldsymbol{\mu}}_t
      \;+\;
      X_t\,\bigl(\widetilde{V}_t\,\mathbf{Z}_t\bigr)
    \Bigr\}^\top
    \,\frac{\partial \boldsymbol{\pi}_\theta}{\partial \theta}
  \,dt
\Bigr],
\label{eq:grad_theta_expanded_zeroindexed}
\\[4pt]
\nabla_{\phi}J
&=\;
\mathbb{E}\!\Bigl[
  \int_0^T
    \lambda_t\,\bigl(-\,\tfrac{\partial C_\phi}{\partial \phi}\bigr)
  \,dt
\Bigr]
\;+\;
\mathbb{E}\!\Bigl[
  \int_0^T
    e^{-\rho t}\,U'\bigl(C_\phi(\cdot)\bigr)\,
    \tfrac{\partial C_\phi(\cdot)}{\partial \phi}
  \,dt
\Bigr].
\label{eq:grad_phi_expanded_zeroindexed}
\end{align}
Because $\frac{\partial \sigma}{\partial \phi}=0$ in this Merton-like setup, the consumption gradient has two clear terms: a \emph{wealth sensitivity} part ($-\lambda_t\,\partial_\phi C_\phi$) and a \emph{direct utility} part ($e^{-\rho t}U'(C_\phi)\,\partial_\phi C_\phi$). 

Equations \eqref{eq:grad_theta_expanded_zeroindexed}--\eqref{eq:grad_phi_expanded_zeroindexed} illustrate how the adjoint variables $(\lambda_t,\mathbf{Z}_t)$, obtained via automatic differentiation, dictate the directions to update $(\theta,\phi)$. As $(\boldsymbol{\pi}_\theta,C_\phi)$ approach the Hamiltonian maximizer under $\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}$, these gradients vanish, recovering classical Merton solutions (unconstrained) or constrained analogs (when barrier/KKT terms are present). Thus, neural network training aligns with Pontryagin's principle, without requiring explicit BSDE solvers. Under standard Lipschitz/integrability assumptions, this approach readily scales to large-scale portfolios while retaining theoretical consistency.















\section{Gradient-Based Algorithm for Policy Optimization}
\label{sec:BPTT_multiasset}

Thus far, we have shown how Pontryagin's Maximum Principle (PMP) provides a forward--backward 
characterization of the optimal solution in both \emph{unconstrained} and \emph{constrained} 
(high-dimensional) Merton problems. However, translating these continuous-time PMP conditions 
into practice requires numerical approximations. We adopt a \emph{gradient-based} scheme 
that leverages backpropagation through time (BPTT) to iteratively refine a 
neural-network policy. This approach extends the one-dimensional methods of 
\citet{huh2024pontryagin}---who introduced the PG-DPO algorithm in a 1D Merton setting---to 
accommodate \emph{multi-asset} portfolios, as well as the \emph{barrier} framework for 
enforcing nonnegative weights and sum-to-one constraints.

Concretely, we parametrize both the \emph{consumption} and \emph{investment} decisions by 
neural networks, then compute a suboptimal \emph{costate} (adjoint) process via BPTT under 
the current policy parameters. This costate informs how to adjust the policy in each gradient 
step to reduce the discrepancy between the induced Hamiltonian and its local Pontryagin maximum. 
In the \emph{unconstrained} case, the training simply proceeds via direct policy gradients, 
while in the \emph{constrained} case, we embed constraints through network activations 
(e.g.\ softmax for nonnegative weights). 

Additionally, we introduce a \emph{OneShot} procedure that directly extracts near-optimal 
Pontryagin controls from the BPTT-derived adjoint, potentially bypassing further policy 
training at test time. Specifically, if the adjoint (\(\lambda_t,\mathbf{Z}_t\)) converges 
quickly, one can deploy ``OneShot'' Pontryagin rules (either a closed-form formula in 
unconstrained Merton or a barrier-based Newton step in the constrained case), thus ignoring 
the network outputs at inference. This can significantly reduce the total training overhead 
for large-scale portfolios.

Hence, the methods in this section form a \emph{Pontryagin-Guided Direct Policy Optimization 
(PG-DPO)} family with two main variants:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{PG-DPO} (baseline), which trains the neural policy end-to-end and deploys it 
      directly (Section~\ref{sec:discrete_algo_multiasset}). 
\item \textbf{PG-DPO-OneShot}, which relies on a brief ``warm-up'' training phase, then 
      applies Pontryagin controls via a closed-form or barrier-based solve at test time  (Section~\ref{sec:pgdpo_oneshot}).
\end{itemize}
Compared to classical dynamic programming or PDE grids, both PG-DPO variants are readily 
scalable to multi-asset, high-dimensional settings, thanks to efficient BPTT and GPU 
implementations. In the remainder of this section, we detail how the forward--backward 
neural policy training works (\S\ref{sec:discrete_algo_multiasset}), and how the OneShot 
procedure integrates with unconstrained or barrier-based constraints 
(\S\ref{sec:pgdpo_oneshot}).







\subsection{Single-Path Approach for Estimating Adjoint Processes}
\label{sec:single_path_approach_multiasset}

A key insight of our method is that one forward simulation (or single path) can produce an
unbiased estimate of the adjoint processes \(\lambda_t\) and \(\mathbf{Z}_t\) at the
visited time--state points \(\bigl(t_k,\,X_k\bigr)\). Specifically,
\(\lambda_t = \tfrac{\partial J}{\partial X_t}\) captures how marginal variations in
the scalar wealth process \(X_t\) affect the objective \(J\bigl(\boldsymbol{\pi}_t,\,C_t\bigr)\). In a
\emph{multi-asset} setting, \(\mathbf{Z}_t \in \mathbb{R}^n\) similarly encodes how the
noise directions in \(\mathbf{W}_t\) propagate through the policy's diffusion terms. Once
we differentiate the policy networks and the forward simulation via automatic
differentiation, we can retrieve consistent estimates of \(\lambda_t\) and \(\mathbf{Z}_t\)
by backpropagating from the scalar objective \(J\bigl(\boldsymbol{\pi}_t,\,C_t\bigr)\). Although the precise
mapping between \(\lambda_t,\mathbf{Z}_t\) and the policy variables can differ across
problem dimensions and parameterizations of \(\Sigma\), the overarching idea remains: a
single trajectory suffices to yield a pathwise (unbiased) estimate of the adjoint processes
at each point \(\bigl(t_k,\,X_k\bigr)\).

This single-path approach has several attractive properties. First, it is unbiased in the
sense that, over many random draws, the average of the estimated adjoints converges to the
true costates in expectation \citep{kushner2003stochastic,borkar2008stochastic}. Second, it
is memory-efficient because each node \(\bigl(t_k,X_k\bigr)\) is visited by exactly one
trajectory (or one mini-batch), avoiding the need to store large datasets or ensembles.
Third, it is online-adaptive in that, as \(\theta,\phi\) are updated, new samples are drawn
in real time, ensuring the policy remains well-trained for the state distribution induced
by its latest parameters.

On the other hand, using only a single sample per node can lead to higher variance in
gradient estimates, potentially slowing or destabilizing learning. 
Section~\ref{sec:pgdpo_oneshot} shows how we can leverage the adjoint estimates directly through a
\emph{OneShot} approach, bypassing the learned policy for faster inference if needed.




\subsection{Discrete-Time Algorithm for Gradient Computation}
\label{sec:discrete_algo_multiasset}

We now detail a concrete procedure, in discrete time, for computing both the adjoint processes 
\(\bigl(\lambda_{t},\mathbf{Z}_{t}\bigr)\) and the parameter gradients \(\bigl(\nabla_{\theta}J,\nabla_{\phi}J\bigr)\) 
under the multi-asset Merton model. This algorithm implements 
backpropagation-through-time (BPTT), jointly handling consumption and multi-dimensional investment 
decisions. Concretely, the following steps outline the core of our PG-DPO approach in discrete time:



\paragraph{(a) Choose Final Activations for Network Constraints.}
\noindent
When designing the neural networks \(\boldsymbol{\pi}_\theta\) and \(C_\phi\), one can 
\emph{directly} enforce constraints on the controls via their final-layer activations:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \emph{Unconstrained:} Simply output real-valued coordinates; no explicit activation 
  is needed.
  \item \emph{No Borrowing / Short-Selling:} Use a \(\mathrm{softmax}\) of length \(n+1\) 
  to ensure \(\boldsymbol{\pi}_k \ge \mathbf{0}\) and \(\sum_{i=0}^n \pi_{i,k} = 1\).
  \item \emph{Consumption Bounds:} If \(0 \le C_k \le \alpha\,X_k\) must hold, a scaled 
  \(\mathrm{sigmoid}\) final activation can keep \(C_k\) within that range. 
\end{itemize}
Such architectural choices make the policy outputs automatically satisfy their respective 
constraints at each time step, minimizing the need for separate penalty or barrier terms.

\paragraph{(b) Discretize Dynamics and Objective.}
\noindent
Partition the time interval \([0,T]\) into \(N\) steps of size 
\(\Delta t = T/N\). Define \(t_k = k\,\Delta t\) for \(k=0,\dots,N\), so that 
\(t_0=0\) and \(t_N=T\). We approximate the continuous-time SDE in \eqref{eq:FSDE} via an 
\emph{exponential Euler} scheme that preserves the geometric nature of wealth updates. 
Concretely, we \emph{freeze} the controls \(\bigl(\boldsymbol{\pi}_t,\,C_t\bigr)\) on each 
interval \(\bigl[t_k,\,t_k + \Delta t\bigr]\). Here we let
\[
  \boldsymbol{\pi}_k 
  \;=\; 
  \boldsymbol{\pi}_\theta\bigl(t_k,\,X_k\bigr),
  \quad
  C_k 
  \;=\; 
  C_\phi\bigl(t_k,\,X_k\bigr).
\]
Applying It\^o's lemma to \(\ln(X_s)\) over \(\bigl[t_k,\,t_k + \Delta t\bigr]\) yields 
the local exponential solution
\[
  X_{k+1}
  \;=\;
  X_k \exp\!\Bigl(
    \Bigl[
      \boldsymbol{\pi}_k^\top\,\widetilde{\boldsymbol{\mu}}_{k}
      \;-\;
      \tfrac12\,\boldsymbol{\pi}_{1:n,k}^\top\,\Sigma_{k}\,\boldsymbol{\pi}_{1:n,k}
      \;-\;
      \tfrac{C_k}{\,X_k\,}
    \Bigr]\,\Delta t
    \;+\;
    \boldsymbol{\pi}_k^\top\,\widetilde{V}_k
    \,\Delta \mathbf{W}_k
  \Bigr),
\]
where
\(\boldsymbol{\pi}_{1:n,k}\) is the sub-vector \(\bigl(\pi_{1,k},\dots,\pi_{n,k}\bigr)\), 
\(\Sigma_{k} = V_{k}\,V_{k}^\top\), and 
\(\Delta \mathbf{W}_k = \mathbf{W}_{t_k+\Delta t}-\mathbf{W}_{t_k}\sim 
\mathcal{N}\bigl(\mathbf{0},\,\Delta t\,I_n\bigr)\). As before, 
\(\widetilde{\boldsymbol{\mu}}_k\in\mathbb{R}^{n+1}\) collects 
\(\bigl(r_{t_k},\,\mu_{1,t_k},\dots,\mu_{n,t_k}\bigr)\), and 
\(\widetilde{V}_k \in \mathbb{R}^{(n+1)\times n}\) encodes a zero row for the risk-free asset 
plus the factorization of \(\Sigma_{k}\). This exponential step can be iterated to 
approximate the continuous-time dynamics. Our goal is to choose parameters 
\((\theta,\phi)\) so as to maximize \(J(\theta,\phi)\).%

\paragraph{(c) Single Forward Path per \(\bigl(t_k,X_k\bigr)\).}
\noindent
At each node \(\bigl(t_k,X_k\bigr)\), we run exactly one forward simulation from \(k\) 
to the terminal index \(N\). This yields a single-sample payoff, unbiased but subject 
to Monte Carlo variance. After the simulation, backpropagation (autodiff) yields 
local adjoint estimates \(\lambda_k\) and \(\mathbf{Z}_k\) under the current policy 
\(\bigl(\theta,\phi\bigr)\).

\paragraph{(d) Compute \(\lambda_k\) via BPTT.}
\noindent
In typical deep-learning frameworks (e.g., \texttt{PyTorch} or JAX), we build a 
computational graph from \(\bigl(\theta,\phi\bigr)\) through 
\(\{\boldsymbol{\pi}_k,\,C_k\}\) and \(\{X_k\}\) to the approximate objective 
\(J(\theta,\phi)\). A single call to \texttt{.backward()} (or an equivalent AD routine) 
computes \(\nabla_\theta J\) and \(\nabla_\phi J\), as well as the partial derivatives 
of \(J\) w.r.t.\ each \(X_k\). Identifying
\[
\lambda_k 
\;=\; 
\frac{\partial J}{\,\partial X_k\,}
\]
aligns with the Pontryagin perspective that \(\lambda_k\) is the (suboptimal) adjoint 
measuring the sensitivity of the overall cost to changes in \(X_k\).

\paragraph{(e) Obtain \(\partial_{x}\lambda_k\) and hence \(\mathbf{Z}_k\).}
\noindent
To compute \(\mathbf{Z}_k\), one typically needs \(\partial_{x}\lambda_k\). In the multi-asset 
zero-indexed Merton model, a common approach differentiates the Hamiltonian or uses the relation
\[
\mathbf{Z}_k 
\;\approx\; 
\bigl[\partial_{x}\lambda_k\bigr]\,
\Bigl(X_k\,\widetilde{V}_k^\top\,\boldsymbol{\pi}_k\Bigr).
\]
Although this step does not \emph{impose} optimality, it provides additional derivatives 
needed for constructing the exact parameter gradients (cf.\ 
\eqref{eq:grad_theta_symbolic_zeroindexed}--\eqref{eq:grad_phi_symbolic_zeroindexed}).

\paragraph{(f) Update Network Parameters.}
\noindent
Finally, we collect \(\nabla_\theta J\) and \(\nabla_\phi J\) and update 
\((\theta,\phi)\) using a stochastic optimizer (e.g., Adam or SGD). In discrete time, 
one typically sums the integrands over \(k=0,\dots,N-1\) with a factor of \(\Delta t\). 
For instance, the expanded \(\nabla_\theta J\) might be approximated by
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:grad_theta_discrete}
\nabla_{\theta}J 
\;\approx\;
\mathbb{E}\Bigl[
  \sum_{k=0}^{N-1}
    \Bigl\{
      \lambda_k\,X_k\,\widetilde{\boldsymbol{\mu}}_k
      \;+\;
      X_k\,\bigl(\widetilde{V}_k\,\mathbf{Z}_k\bigr)
    \Bigr\}^\top
    \!\frac{\partial\,\boldsymbol{\pi}_\theta\!\bigl(t_k,\,X_k\bigr)}{\partial \theta}
  \;\Delta t
\Bigr],
\end{equation}
while the expanded \(\nabla_\phi J\) might be approximated by
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:grad_phi_discrete}
\nabla_{\phi}J
\;\approx\;
\mathbb{E}\Bigl[
  \sum_{k=0}^{N-1}
    \lambda_k\,\Bigl(-\tfrac{\partial\,C_\phi\!\bigl(t_k,\,X_k\bigr)}{\partial \phi}\Bigr)
  \;\Delta t
\Bigr]
\;+\;
\mathbb{E}\Bigl[
  \sum_{k=0}^{N-1}
    e^{-\rho\,t_k}\,U'\!\bigl(C_k\bigr)
    \,\frac{\partial\,C_\phi\!\bigl(t_k,\,X_k\bigr)}{\partial \phi}
  \;\Delta t
\Bigr].
\end{equation}
Averaging these over \(M\) trajectories (or mini-batches) yields a stochastic approximation 
of \(\nabla_\theta J\) and \(\nabla_\phi J\). Repeating this process eventually produces 
a stationary policy.

\bigskip

In practice, one could simply define the neural policy \(\bigl(\boldsymbol{\pi}_\theta,\,C_\phi\bigr)\), compute \(J(\theta,\phi)\) on simulated trajectories, and call \texttt{.backward()}; automatic differentiation then handles the internal adjoint logic. Nonetheless, explicitly recognizing \(\bigl(\lambda_k,\mathbf{Z}_k\bigr)\) as suboptimal Pontryagin adjoints clarifies why backpropagation works and how to incorporate additional alignment or constraints. Altogether, these steps form the foundation of our PG-DPO scheme in the multi-asset Merton setting.







\subsection{OneShot PG-DPO with BPTT-Derived Adjoint}
\label{sec:pgdpo_oneshot}

One key insight of our approach is that the adjoint (costate) process \(\lambda_t\) and 
its derivative \(\partial_x \lambda_t\) can converge relatively quickly under backpropagation-through-time 
(BPTT), even if the policy networks \(\bigl(\boldsymbol{\pi}_\theta,C_\phi\bigr)\) remain suboptimal. 
This observation motivates a \emph{OneShot} procedure, which derives near-optimal controls 
\(\bigl(\boldsymbol{\pi}^{\mathrm{PMP}},\,C^{\mathrm{PMP}}\bigr)\) directly from 
\(\bigl(\lambda_t,\partial_x \lambda_t\bigr)\), thereby bypassing the neural-network outputs at test time. 
Below, we outline how this works in both \emph{unconstrained} and \emph{constrained} settings.


\medskip
\noindent
\textbf{(a) Unconstrained OneShot.}
If the problem imposes \emph{no} constraints on borrowing or consumption, the adjoint 
\(\bigl(\lambda_k,\partial_x \lambda_k\bigr)\) can be plugged into a closed-form Pontryagin solution.  
One obtains the continuous-time first-order conditions 
\eqref{eq:C_opt_unconstrained} and \eqref{eq:pi_opt_unconstrained} from Pontryagin's Maximum 
Principle.  When these expressions are \emph{discretized} in time, they yield a rule such as
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:PMP_controls}
\begin{aligned}
C^{\mathrm{PMP}}_k
&\;=\;
\Bigl(e^{\rho\,t_k}\,\lambda_k\Bigr)^{-\tfrac{1}{\gamma}},\\
\boldsymbol{\pi}_{1:n,k}^{\mathrm{PMP}}
&\;=\;
-\,\frac{\lambda_k}{\,X_k\,\bigl(\partial_x \lambda_k\bigr)\,}
\;\Sigma_{k}^{-1}\!\bigl(\mu_{1,k}-r_{k},\;\dots,\;\mu_{n,k}-r_{k}\bigr).
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
Hence, once BPTT has produced sufficiently accurate \(\lambda_k\approx \tfrac{\partial J}{\partial X_k}\) 
and \(\partial_x \lambda_k\), we can immediately compute 
\(\bigl(\boldsymbol{\pi}_k^{\mathrm{PMP}},\,C_k^{\mathrm{PMP}}\bigr)\) 
\emph{in one shot} without further network training.  This approach can dramatically reduce 
computational cost if \eqref{eq:PMP_controls} is available.




\medskip
\noindent
\textbf{(b) Constrained OneShot (Barrier Method).}
In the presence of constraints (e.g., no short-selling or consumption bounds), 
we no longer have a simple closed-form from the Pontryagin FOCs. Moreover, 
a KKT-based method can be computationally expensive due to large, piecewise systems.  
Instead, we adopt a \emph{barrier-based} approach, quickly solving a small-scale system at 
each node \(\bigl(t_k,X_k,\lambda_k,\partial_x \lambda_k\bigr)\).  Recall the \emph{barrier} 
Hamiltonian
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:Hamiltonian_barrier}
\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{\mathrm{barrier}}
\;=\;
\mathcal{H}
\;+\;
\eta_t \Bigl(1 - \sum_{i=0}^n \pi_{i,t}\Bigr)
\;+\;
\epsilon \sum_{i=0}^n \ln\!\bigl(\pi_{i,t}\bigr),
\end{equation}
where \(\eta_t\) enforces \(\sum_{i=0}^n \pi_{i,t}=1\) and the log-barrier 
\(\epsilon \sum_{i=0}^n \ln(\pi_{i,t})\) ensures \(\pi_{i,t}>0\).  
Once BPTT provides \(\lambda_k\) and $\partial_x \lambda_k$, we incorporate them into 
\(\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{\mathrm{barrier}}\) and \emph{locally} solve 
\(\max_{\boldsymbol{\pi}_k,\,C_k}\,\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{\mathrm{barrier}}\)
(e.g., via Newton--line-search of Section~\ref{sec:barrier_sum1_nosell_Zsub_sigma}) to obtain 
\(\bigl(\boldsymbol{\pi}_k^{\mathrm{PMP}},\,C_k^{\mathrm{PMP}}\bigr)\).  
Because this is only a low-dimensional solve (roughly \(n+2\) if we count the multiplier 
\(\eta_t\)), it is typically much faster than retraining the entire network.  Thus, even with 
constraints, we can treat \(\lambda_k\) and $\partial_x \lambda_k$ as a ``shortcut'' for computing 
OneShot controls at each \(\bigl(t_k,X_k\bigr)\).

\medskip
\noindent
\textbf{(c) Workflow of OneShot.}
In practice, we proceed as follows:
\begin{enumerate}%[label=(\alph*)]
  \item \textit{Warm up:} Partially train \(\bigl(\boldsymbol{\pi}_\theta,C_\phi\bigr)\) so that 
        \(\lambda_t\) and \(\mathbf{Z}_t\) become reasonably stable under BPTT.
  \item \textit{Extract:} On the desired test scenario, forward-simulate to obtain 
        \(\lambda_k,\mathbf{Z}_k\) at each node \(\bigl(t_k,X_k\bigr)\).
  \item \textit{Compute OneShot Controls:}
    \begin{itemize}
      \item \textit{Unconstrained:} Use a closed-form FOC \eqref{eq:PMP_controls} if available.
      \item \textit{Constrained:} Solve the barrier system \eqref{eq:Hamiltonian_barrier} 
            to find \(\bigl(\boldsymbol{\pi}_k^{\mathrm{PMP}},C_k^{\mathrm{PMP}}\bigr)\).
    \end{itemize}
  \item \textit{Deploy:} The resulting \(\bigl(\boldsymbol{\pi}_k^{\mathrm{PMP}},C_k^{\mathrm{PMP}}\bigr)\) 
        can be used at test time, ignoring the original policy network outputs. 
        In many cases, this drastically reduces the remaining training cost while 
        still achieving near-optimal performance.
\end{enumerate}

Although OneShot does not produce a final policy network for direct deployment, it remains 
highly flexible. Indeed, one can simply run a brief forward pass and backprop for each 
new initial state, using costate-based formulas or barrier solves to generate near-optimal 
controls without further network updates. Numerical experiments (Section~\ref{sec:num_test}) 
confirm that OneShot can substantially reduce training effort, achieving near-optimal performance 
even with a moderate warm-up phase, whether constraints are present or not.










\subsection{Extended Value Function and Algorithmic Variants}
\label{sec:extended_value_function}

\subsubsection{Extended Value Function and Discretized Rollouts}
\label{sec:algo_variants:extended_value}

In Section~\ref{sec:discrete_algo_multiasset}, we introduced
a discretization scheme for the Merton problem over a fixed interval
\([0,T]\). Here, we extend that approach to handle any initial time
\(t_{0}\) and wealth \(x_{0}\) by defining an extended value function
over a broader domain.

Many continuous-time problems (including Merton) require a policy
\(\bigl(\boldsymbol{\pi}_{\theta},C_{\phi}\bigr)\) valid for any initial condition
\(\bigl(t_{0},x_{0}\bigr)\). To address this requirement, we define an
\emph{extended value function} that integrates over random initial nodes
\(\bigl(t_{0},x_{0}\bigr)\sim\eta(\cdot)\) in the domain
\(\mathcal{D}\subset[0,T]\times(0,\infty)\), and then discretize each resulting
trajectory. Here, \(\eta\) denotes the distribution of
\(\bigl(t_{0},x_{0}\bigr)\). Concretely, we set
\[
\widehat{J}(\theta,\phi)
=\;
\mathbb{E}_{(t_{0},x_{0})\,\sim\,\eta}
\Bigl[
  \mathbb{E}\Bigl(\,
    \int_{t_{0}}^{T} e^{-\rho u}\,U\bigl(C_{\phi}(u,X_{u})\bigr)\,du
    \;+\;
    \kappa\,e^{-\rho T}\,U\bigl(X_{T}\bigr)
  \Bigr)
\Bigr].
\]
Hence, maximizing \(\widehat{J}(\theta,\phi)\) yields a policy
\(\bigl(\boldsymbol{\pi}_{\theta},C_{\phi}\bigr)\) that applies across all
sub-intervals \([t_{0},T]\) and initial wealth \(x_{0}\).

To avoid overfitting to a single initial scenario, we draw
\(\bigl(t_{0},x_{0}\bigr)\) from a suitable distribution \(\eta\). By doing so,
the policy \(\bigl(\boldsymbol{\pi}_{\theta},C_{\phi}\bigr)\) is trained to cover
a broad region of the time--wealth domain rather than just one initial
condition. This sampling strategy is standard in many PDE-based or RL-like
continuous-time methods that need a single control law
\(\bigl(\boldsymbol{\pi}_{\theta},C_{\phi}\bigr)\) covering all
\(\bigl(t,x\bigr)\).

To implement the integral in \(\widehat{J}(\theta,\phi)\), one can follow
the step-by-step procedure in
Algorithm~\ref{algo:pgdpo_no_reg_multiasset}, which details how to discretize the
interval, simulate each path, and apply backpropagation to update
\(\bigl(\theta,\phi\bigr)\).

\subsubsection{Gradient-Based Algorithmic Variants}
\label{sec:algo_variants_multiasset_2}

Having introduced the extended value function \(\widehat{J}(\theta,\phi)\) over
random initial nodes \(\bigl(t_0,x_0\bigr)\), we now present two main
PG-DPO methods for the multi-asset Merton problem. Both rely on the same procedure
of simulating from sampled initial conditions and computing a stochastic
estimate of \(\widehat{J}(\theta,\phi)\). They differ in whether we \emph{deploy 
the learned networks} or a \emph{one-shot Pontryagin control} at test time.  
When constraints such as no short-selling or consumption bounds are present, 
we handle them either by final-layer activations in the policy nets (Section~\ref{sec:discrete_algo_multiasset}) 
or by a barrier-based one-shot solution (Sections~\ref{sec:barrier_sum1_nosell_Zsub_sigma} and \ref{sec:pgdpo_oneshot}).



\begin{algorithm}
\caption{\textbf{PG-DPO}}
\label{algo:pgdpo_no_reg_multiasset}

\textbf{Inputs:} 
\begin{itemize}
\item Policy nets \(\bigl(\boldsymbol{\pi}_\theta,\,C_\phi\bigr)\) 
   (optionally with constraint-enforcing final activations);
\item Step sizes \(\{\alpha_k\}\), total iterations \(K\);
\item Domain sampler \(\eta\) for \(\bigl(t_0^{(i)},\,x_0^{(i)}\bigr)\) in 
   \(\mathcal{D}\subset [0,T]\times (0,\infty)\);
\item Integer \(N\) (steps per path).
\end{itemize}

\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\FOR{\(j=1\) to \(K\)}

\STATE \textbf{(a) Sample mini-batch of size \(M\).} For each \(i\in\{1,\dots,M\}\), draw 
\(\bigl(t_0^{(i)},\,x_0^{(i)}\bigr)\sim \eta\).

\STATE \textbf{(b) Local Single-Path Simulation.} For each \(i\):
  \begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*)]
  \item \(\Delta t^{(i)} \,\leftarrow\,\tfrac{T - t_0^{(i)}}{N}\); 
        \(\,X_0^{(i)} \leftarrow x_0^{(i)}\).
  \item For \(k=0,\dots,N-1\):
    \[
      \boldsymbol{\pi}_k^{(i)} 
      = 
      \boldsymbol{\pi}_\theta(t_k^{(i)},X_k^{(i)}),
      \;
      C_k^{(i)} 
      = 
      C_\phi(t_k^{(i)},X_k^{(i)}),
    \]
  \[
\begin{aligned}
  X_{k+1}^{(i)}
  &= 
  X_k^{(i)} \exp\!\Bigl(
    \Bigl[
      \bigl(\boldsymbol{\pi}_k^{(i)}\bigr)^\top\widetilde{\boldsymbol{\mu}}_k^{(i)}
      \;-\;
      \tfrac12\,\bigl(\boldsymbol{\pi}_{1:n,k}^{(i)}\bigr)^\top\,\Sigma_k^{(i)}\,\boldsymbol{\pi}_{1:n,k}^{(i)}
      \;-\;
      \tfrac{\,C_k^{(i)}\,}{\,X_k^{(i)}\,}
    \Bigr]\Delta t^{(i)}
  \\[6pt]
  &\hphantom{= X_k^{(i)} \exp\!\Bigl( }
    \qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad \;+\;
    \bigl(\boldsymbol{\pi}_k^{(i)}\bigr)^\top\,\widetilde{V}_k^{(i)}
    \,\Delta \mathbf{W}_k^{(i)}
  \Bigr).
\end{aligned}
\]

  \item Compute 
    \[
    J^{(i)}(\theta,\phi)
    =
    \sum_{k=0}^{N-1} e^{-\rho\,t_k^{(i)}}\,
      U\bigl(C_k^{(i)}\bigr)\,\Delta t^{(i)}
    \;+\;
    \kappa\,e^{-\rho\,T}\,U\bigl(X_N^{(i)}\bigr).
    \]
  \end{enumerate}

\STATE \textbf{(c) Backprop \& Averaging:} 
\[
\widehat{J}(\theta,\phi)
=\frac{1}{M}\sum_{i=1}^M J^{(i)}(\theta,\phi),
\quad
\nabla_{(\theta,\phi)}\widehat{J}
\leftarrow\text{BPTT.}
\]

\STATE \textbf{(d) Parameter Update:}
\[
(\theta,\phi)
\;\leftarrow\;
(\theta,\phi)
+ 
\alpha_k\,\nabla_{(\theta,\phi)}\widehat{J}.
\]

\ENDFOR

\RETURN \(\bigl(\boldsymbol{\pi}_\theta,\,C_\phi\bigr)\).

\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}




\begin{algorithm}[t!]
\caption{\textbf{PG-DPO-OneShot}}
\label{algo:pgdpo_oneshot}

\textbf{Additional Inputs:}
\begin{itemize}
  \item A brief ``warm-up'' phase (e.g., \(K_0\) iterations of PG-DPO).
  \item Suboptimal adjoint \(\lambda_k\) and its spatial derivative 
        \(\tfrac{\partial}{\partial x}\lambda_k\), \emph{both} obtained via automatic 
        differentiation (BPTT) for each node \(\bigl(t_k,X_k\bigr)\).
\end{itemize}

\begin{algorithmic}[1]

\STATE \textbf{(a) Warm-Up Training:}
  \begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*),leftmargin=1.5em]
    \item Run PG-DPO for \(K_0\) iterations.  Although \(\boldsymbol{\pi}_\theta,\,C_\phi\) 
          may remain suboptimal, the costate \(\lambda_t \approx \tfrac{\partial J}{\partial X_t}\) 
          typically stabilizes quickly under BPTT.
    \item After warm-up, at each node \(\bigl(t_k,X_k\bigr)\), retrieve 
          \(\lambda_k\) and \(\tfrac{\partial}{\partial x}\lambda_k\) from autodiff.
  \end{enumerate}

\STATE \textbf{(b) OneShot Pontryagin Controls (Unconstrained vs.\ Barrier).}
  \begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*),leftmargin=1.5em]
    \item \emph{If unconstrained}, apply a closed-form Pontryagin FOC 
    \(\bigl(\boldsymbol{\pi}_k^{\mathrm{PMP}},\,C_k^{\mathrm{PMP}}\bigr)\) 
    (e.g.\ \eqref{eq:PMP_controls} in a multi-asset Merton model).
    \item \emph{If constraints exist}, solve 
    \(\max_{\boldsymbol{\pi}_k,C_k}\,\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{\mathrm{barrier}}\)
    (see \eqref{eq:Hamiltonian_barrier}) 
    via a small-scale \emph{barrier-based} Newton--line-search at 
    \(\bigl(t_k,X_k,\lambda_k,\tfrac{\partial}{\partial x}\lambda_k\bigr)\).  
    Return \(\bigl(\boldsymbol{\pi}_k^{\mathrm{PMP}},\,C_k^{\mathrm{PMP}}\bigr)\).  
  \end{enumerate}

\STATE \textbf{(c) Deploy OneShot Controls:}
  \begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*),leftmargin=1.5em]
  \item At test time, \emph{ignore} the network outputs 
        \(\bigl(\boldsymbol{\pi}_\theta,\,C_\phi\bigr)\).  Use 
        \(\bigl(\boldsymbol{\pi}_k^{\mathrm{PMP}},\,C_k^{\mathrm{PMP}}\bigr)\) 
        from step (c) instead.
  \item This requires only a short warm-up plus local solves (closed-form or barrier).  
        Experiments (Section~\ref{sec:num_test}) confirm near-optimal solutions 
        with significantly reduced training cost.
  \end{enumerate}

\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}








\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*)]

\item \textbf{PG-DPO (Baseline).}\\
This is the \emph{basic} scheme, analogous to 
Algorithm~\ref{algo:pgdpo_no_reg_multiasset}. It optimizes \(\widehat{J}\)
directly via backpropagation-through-time (BPTT), returning a final policy 
\(\bigl(\boldsymbol{\pi}_\theta,\,C_\phi\bigr)\).  
In unconstrained settings, the network can produce any real values; 
in constrained scenarios (e.g.\ sum-to-one, no negative weights), 
we can enforce them with softmax or scaled sigmoids.  
Empirically, PG-DPO converges reliably toward Pontryagin-like 
solutions, albeit with potentially slower or noisier training as dimension grows.

\item \textbf{PG-DPO-OneShot.}\\
A second variant uses the fact that the costate (adjoint) \(\lambda_t\)
may converge faster than the policy networks themselves. After a short warm-up
phase (running PG-DPO for a few iterations), we extract 
\(\lambda_k \approx \tfrac{\partial J}{\partial X_k}\) and 
\(\partial_x \lambda_k\) from BPTT. We then compute
\(\bigl(\boldsymbol{\pi}_k^{\mathrm{PMP}}, C_k^{\mathrm{PMP}}\bigr)\) using either a 
\emph{closed-form Pontryagin rule} (unconstrained Merton) or a \emph{barrier-based}
Newton solve (\S\ref{sec:barrier_sum1_nosell_Zsub_sigma}). At test time, we
\emph{ignore} the neural-network outputs and directly deploy those one-shot
Pontryagin controls. This drastically reduces training requirements by
bypassing final network inference, though it does not produce a 
``ready-to-deploy'' policy net for real-time usage.

\end{enumerate}

Both methods extend naturally to the multi-asset case by leveraging Pontryagin's principle 
(Section~\ref{sec:suboptimal_policies_multiasset}) and the barrier approach if constraints are 
involved (Section~\ref{sec:barrier_sum1_nosell_Zsub_sigma}).  One typically samples 
\(\bigl(t_0, x_0\bigr)\) from a chosen distribution \(\eta\) to cover various times and wealth 
levels, and then computes \(\lambda_0\) (and \(\tfrac{\partial \lambda_0}{\partial x}\)) for 
each sample. A detailed proof of convergence in a single-asset setting can be found in 
\citet{huh2024pontryagin}; although restricted to one dimension, it extends to multi-asset 
problems under mild assumptions (e.g.\ Lipschitz continuity of drift/diffusion, differentiability 
of the policy nets).








\section{Numerical Results}\label{sec:num_test}

In this section, we illustrate how our Pontryagin-Guided Direct Policy Optimization
(PG-DPO) framework applies to a \emph{multi-asset} Merton problem that integrates both
portfolio allocation and continuous consumption. While many recent deep learning
approaches to stochastic control---including deep BSDE methods
\citep[e.g.,][]{han2018solving,e2017deep}, physics-informed neural networks \citep{raissi2019physics},
and model-free reinforcement learning \citep[e.g.,][]{reppen2023deep_df,reppen2023deep_mf,dai2023learning}---have
shown promise in lower-dimensional or simpler PDE settings, we focus on the large-scale,
continuous-time setting with potentially thousands of risky assets.

We consider two main scenarios. First, in Section~\ref{sec:unconstrained_test}, we examine the \emph{standard Merton problem} under an \emph{unconstrained} setting with constant drift and volatility (i.e., a constant opportunity set).
In this classical setup, a \emph{closed-form solution} is well-known to exist, which allows us to directly compare PG-DPO and its one-shot variant against the analytical optimum as the number of risky assets grows.
Second, in Section~\ref{sec:constrained_test}, we add \emph{no short-selling or borrowing} constraints. Because no closed-form solution is available under these constraints, we measure the residual in the first-order optimality conditions (FOCs) as our accuracy metric.
Before discussing each scenario in detail, Section~\ref{sec:exp_setup} describes our experimental setup, including parameter choices, neural network architectures, and training procedures.



\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:exp_setup}

We consider an investment horizon up to \(T = 1\) and restrict the wealth process \(X_t\) to lie in \([0.1,\,2]\). Unlike the earlier approach that used a random correlation matrix, we now \emph{generate the market parameters} \(\bigl(\mu, \Sigma\bigr)\) for the risky assets using a CAPM-based procedure with optional Ledoit--Wolf shrinkage. Specifically, for each multi-asset scenario of dimension \(n \in \{10,\,100,\,1000\}\), we construct:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*)]
\item \textbf{Drift vector.} Under CAPM, each asset \(i\) has a beta \(\beta_i\) drawn from a specified interval (e.g., \([0.8,\,1.2]\)), an idiosyncratic volatility \(\sigma_{\varepsilon,i}\) drawn from \([0.05,\,0.5]\), and a market volatility \(\sigma_M\). Its drift is then
\[
  \mu_i
  \;=\;
  r
  \;+\;
  \beta_i \;\bigl(\mu_M - r\bigr),
\]
where \(\mu_M\) is the market's expected return and \(r\) is the risk-free rate (e.g., \(r=0.03\)).
%
\item \textbf{Covariance matrix.} We model the systematic risk by \(\sigma_M^2\,\beta\,\beta^\top\) (where \(\beta\) is the vector of all assets' betas) and add a diagonal matrix of idiosyncratic variances \(\sigma_{\varepsilon}^2\). The resulting matrix \(S\) may then be shrinkage-adjusted to ensure well-conditioned covariances, yielding
\[
  \Sigma
  \;=\;
  \sigma_M^2\,\beta\,\beta^\top
  \;+\;
  \mathrm{diag}\!\bigl(\sigma_{\varepsilon}^2\bigr)
  \;\;\;\xrightarrow{\text{optional shrinkage}}\;\;
  \Sigma_{\mathrm{final}}.
\]
\end{enumerate}

We fix \(\gamma = 2\) (relative risk aversion) and \(\rho = 0.1\) (discount rate), and we typically use \(m=5\) time steps per simulation rollout. Note that if short-selling or borrowing constraints are imposed, the analytical Merton solution ceases to hold, which motivates the numerical methods presented here.

In each training iteration, we randomly draw \(10^3\) initial states \(\bigl(t_0,\,X_0\bigr)\) in \([0,\,T]\times[0.1,\,2]\). We then partition the interval \([t_0,\,T]\) into \(m\) steps of size \(\Delta t = (T - t_0)/m\) and simulate the wealth process forward via Euler--Maruyama under the current policy. In the unconstrained case, the policy network \(\boldsymbol{\pi}_\theta(t,X)\) outputs real-valued coordinates in \(\mathbb{R}^n\). In the constrained setting, it uses a \(\mathrm{softmax}\) final layer, ensuring \(\boldsymbol{\pi}_\theta(t,X)\in\Delta^n\), i.e., nonnegative weights that sum to 1 (no short-selling or borrowing). The consumption network \(C_\phi(t,X)\) has two hidden layers (Leaky-ReLU activations) and a final \(\mathrm{softplus}\) layer to keep consumption nonnegative.

We train both networks for up to 100k gradient steps using the Adam optimizer (mini-batch size \(10^3\), initial learning rate \(10^{-5}\)). In each iteration, once the simulation (rollout) is complete, we compute the payoff objective (discounted utility of consumption and terminal wealth), backpropagate through time (BPTT), and update the parameters \(\theta\) and \(\phi\). Throughout the experiments, to avoid overfitting to a single dataset, we redraw new initial states \(\bigl(t_0,\,X_0\bigr)\) at each iteration, thereby ensuring broad coverage of the time--wealth domain.

All mean-square-error (MSE) results reported in the subsequent sections are obtained by repeatedly sampling \(\bigl(t_0,X_0\bigr)\) and comparing the learned policies \(\bigl(C_t,\boldsymbol{\pi}_t\bigr)\) to the known analytical solution (in the unconstrained case) or by computing first-order condition residuals (in the constrained case).


