We first evaluate PG-DPO and its one-shot variant (PG-DPO-OneShot) on a multi-asset Merton problem \emph{without} any borrowing or short-selling constraints. As discussed in Section~\ref{sec:closed_form_multiasset}, the unconstrained Merton model admits a closed-form solution for both \emph{investment} (\(\boldsymbol{\pi}_t\)) and \emph{consumption} (\(C_t\)), allowing direct comparison between the learned policies and the true optimum. We test dimensions \(n \in \{10,\,100,\,1000\}\).

Table~\ref{tab:unconstrained} shows the \emph{relative MSE} in consumption and investment at three iteration milestones (\(1\mathrm{k}\), \(10\mathrm{k}\), \(100\mathrm{k}\)). One observes that PG-DPO exhibits substantial residual errors, especially for the \emph{investment} policy, and that these errors do not significantly improve even with further training. Indeed, the MSE often remains near or above \(\mathcal{O}(1)\), making the learned allocations effectively unsuitable for practical use. By contrast, PG-DPO-OneShot reduces both consumption and investment MSE by one to two orders of magnitude, yielding much more accurate controls.

Figures~\ref{fig:consumption_mse} and \ref{fig:investment_mse} plot these MSE trajectories. We see that the standard PG-DPO (blue) lingers at comparatively large errors, especially for investment, whereas PG-DPO-OneShot (red) quickly settles to \(\sim\!10^{-3}\) or below. The key difference is that PG-DPO-OneShot \emph{directly} enforces Pontryagin's FOCs (via the adjoint), rather than indirectly learning them through the network.

