\subsection{Constrained Multi-Asset Case}
\label{sec:constrained_test}

We now consider the multi-asset Merton problem under \emph{no short-selling or borrowing} constraints: each weight \(\pi_{i,t}\) lies in \([0,1]\), and the allocations across all risky assets plus the risk-free asset sum to 1. Importantly, \emph{our framework also incorporates continuous consumption decisions} rather than focusing solely on investment---an aspect often omitted in constraint-based portfolio models due to its added complexity. Nevertheless, we show that the combined consumption--investment problem remains tractable under Pontryagin's principle, even in a high-dimensional, constrained setting.

In principle, \emph{if} a particular constraint becomes active (e.g., \(\pi_{i,t}\) is pinned at 0 for certain \((t,X_t)\)), that asset's proportion is still \emph{constant} in time and wealth---namely, identically zero. Consequently, even with constraints, the classical CRRA intuition of time/wealth-\emph{invariant} proportions can hold so long as each asset's weight remains fixed, whether at zero or another level.


To assess whether the learned policies deviate from this near-constant pattern, we define a CRRA Violation Index:
\[
  \text{CV}_{\mathrm{index}}
  \;=\;
  \sum_{i=1}^{n}
    \,\mathrm{Var}_{(t,X_t)}\bigl(\pi_i(t,X_t)\bigr),
\]
where \(\mathrm{Var}_{(t,X_t)}(\cdot)\) is computed over all sampled \((t,X_t)\) points in the training domain. Intuitively, if each \(\pi_i(t,X_t)\) is genuinely constant (possibly zero, if the constraint binds), then \(\mathrm{Var}_{(t,X_t)}(\pi_i) = 0\). Hence, a higher \(\text{CV}_{\mathrm{index}}\) indicates stronger departures from CRRA's idealized flat profile across time and wealth.

