As a proof of concept, we confined live \textit{P. mirabilis} swarms using optical tweezers and quantified their motility using PMM-OT force measurements~\cite{Farré2017} and high-resolution MPT~\cite{rodriguez2015direct,herraez2020multiple}. Trapped clusters exhibited coordinated, vortex-like motion under confinement —\textit{like a swarm within a hive} (Fig.~\ref{fig:tracking}, Suppl. Movie M1), consistent with active Brownian dynamics~\cite{patteson2018propagation}. From stationary NESS trajectories~\cite{gardiner1985handbook}, we computed motility work directly from center-of-mass displacements and optical forces~\cite{jones2021stochastic}. This stochastic energetics framework captures how internal dissipation sustains collective propulsion against trapping, revealing power–friction imbalances that constrain the thermodynamic efficiency of flagellar cooperativity in fluctuating microenvironments.


