\section{Real Data Experiments}

We evaluate the performance of LDA-GO and its low-rank extension on a collection of six multivariate datasets, from MATLAB built-in (the first three) with low-dimensional features, to academic paper database and gene expression data with high-dimensional features.
%. The next dataset are academic paper database, featuring higher-dimensional sparse representations. The last dataset is gene expression profiles, characterized by extremely high-dimensional features and small sample sizes, making it particularly suitable for assessing the robustness of classification methods under high-dimensional settings.

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Fisher Iris Dataset:} Contains 150 samples with 4 features per instance (sepal length, sepal width, petal length, petal width). Labels correspond to three species of iris flowers: setosa, versicolor, and virginica \citep{fisher1936use}.
\item \textbf{Breast Cancer Wisconsin Dataset:} Consists of 699 instances, each with 9 features extracted from digitized images of fine needle aspirates. The binary class label denotes benign versus malignant tumors \citep{street1993nuclear}.
\item \textbf{Wine Dataset:} Comprises 178 samples with 13 chemical analysis features, describing wines derived from three different cultivars.
\item \textbf{Cora Dataset:} Includes 2708 scientific papers categorized into 7 classes \citep{mccallum2000automating}. Each paper is represented by a 1433-dimensional binary word presence vector.
%\item \textbf{Citeseer Dataset:} Contains 3312 scientific papers classified into 6 classes \citep{giles1998citeseer}. Each sample is a 3703-dimensional binary word occurrence vector.
\item \textbf{Colon Cancer Dataset:} Comprises 62 samples with gene expression profiles measured across 2000 genes. The dataset contains 40 tumor samples and 22 normal tissue samples \citep{alon1999broad}.
\item \textbf{Golub Leukemia Dataset:} Consists of 72 samples, each with expression levels measured across 7129 genes \citep{golub1999molecular}. Five label vectors are available, including tissue origin, gender, source institution, tissue subtype, and cancer type. We carry out the experiment against each label type. 
\end{itemize}

For each dataset, we assess classification performance using 5-fold cross-validation, repeated across $5$ random replicates to compute the mean and standard deviation of the classification error. The results are presented in Table~\ref{tab:data_results}. Overall, both LDA-GO and its low-rank variant exhibit strong performance, often matching or surpassing classical LDA, particularly on high-dimensional, low-sample-size datasets such as Golub. Notably, LDA-GO-d performs comparably to the full-rank version despite using only $d=20$, suggesting that this extension is effective and that a smaller model may suffice in many scenarios.

