\section{Conclusion}

In this paper, we proposed a new approach to estimate the inverse covariance matrix in linear discriminant analysis, based on a combination of multiple initializations, gradient-based optimization on covariance inverse, Cholesky factorization, and low-rank extension. Through extensive simulations and real-data experiments under settings where a linear decision boundary is expected, our method consistently achieved satisfactory results. This study demonstrates that traditional statistical frameworks and modern machine learning techniques can be seamlessly integrated to create more powerful and flexible classifiers.

Looking forward, several promising future directions can be considered. One immediate extension is to apply this framework to a broader range of downstream tasks. In this paper, we primarily focused on classification error and cross-entropy loss; however, the same methodology can naturally be adapted to alternative loss functions. For example, one could treat LDA as a pure dimension reduction technique followed by regression, or extend the framework to any task that leverages the covariance matrix, provided that the objective function remains differentiable with respect to the covariance matrix or its inverse.

Another possible direction is to incorporate a simple dimension reduction step prior to applying LDA-GO, in an effort to further reduce the number of model parameters and speed up the learning process. When the feature dimension $p$ is very large, such a two-stage strategy could significantly reduce computational cost. This is important because, even with the low-rank extension, the current method still involves operations on covariance matrices of size $p \times p$, which can be a bottleneck for very large $p$. 

Another interesting extension is to adapt the gradient-based optimization framework to quadratic discriminant analysis (QDA), where each class is modeled with its own covariance matrix rather than a shared one. However, this direction presents substantial computational challenges: not only does the derivative structure become more complex, but the number of parameters to optimize increases from $\mathcal{O}(pd)$ in LDA to $\mathcal{O}(Kpd)$ in QDA, where $K$ is the number of classes. In this case as well, dimensionality reduction would likely be a beneficial preprocessing step to ease the computational burden.

%Overall, we believe that combining classical statistical insights with modern optimization techniques opens a fruitful avenue for improving discriminative models, particularly in high-dimensional and complex data settings.

