\section{Simulation Setting Details}
\label{sec:sims}

Here we detail the underlying data generation process for each simulation. We always set $n_{\text{test}} = 200$, $n_{\text{test}} = 10000$, $K=2$ with equally likely class prior.

\subsection*{Simulation 1: Multivariate Gaussian without Class Separation}
Let $p = 20$, drawn samples from a standard multivariate normal distribution independently of class labels:
\[
X \sim \mathcal{N}(0, I), \quad Y \sim \text{Bernoulli}(0.5).
\]
This serves as a sanity check where no actual class signal exists. Any classifier should essentially be random guessing.

\subsection*{Simulation 2-4: Separable Classes with Varying Covariance Structure}
Let $p = 20$, each class is drawn from a multivariate normal distribution:
\[
X \mid Y=1 \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \Sigma), \quad X \mid Y=2 \sim \mathcal{N}(1, \Sigma),
\]
where $\Sigma$ is identity matrix for simulation 2; is diagonal with $\Sigma(1,1)=100$, $\Sigma(2,2)=10$, and $1$ on other diagonals for simulation 3; and is randomly generated with $\Sigma=AA'$, where $A$ is p-dimensional random standard Gaussian, for simulation $4$.

This provides some standard, multivariate, Gaussian setting where classical LDA should handle well.

\subsection*{Simulation 5-6: Separable Classes with Toeplitz-Correlated Structure}

Let $p = 200$, generate samples via strongly correlated dimensions, where class separation is dense and uniform across all dimensions.

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Class Means:}
    \[
    \mu_1 = \mathbf{0}_{200}, \quad \mu_2 = \mathbf{1}_{200}.
    \]
    The signal is distributed equally across all dimensions.

    \item \textbf{Covariance Structure:}
    \[
    \Sigma_{ij} = \rho^{|i-j|}, \quad \rho = 0.8,
    \]
    a Toeplitz matrix inducing strong correlations across nearby dimensions.

    \item \textbf{Data Generation:}  
    Training and test sets are sampled from the following:
    \[
    X \mid Y = 1 \sim \mathcal{N}(\mu_1, \Sigma), \quad X \mid Y = 2 \sim \mathcal{N}(\mu_2, \Sigma).
    \]
    Class labels are assigned uniformly at random.
\end{itemize}
For simulation 6, we additionally set $\Sigma(1,1)=100$ and $\Sigma(2,2)=10$ to dominate all other diagonals. These two simulations are designed to challenge classical LDA with a high-dimensional setting with correlated covariance structure. 

%\textbf{Implications:}  
%Classical LDA is prone to failure here due to instability in $\hat{\Sigma}^{-1}$. Naive Bayes or k-NN underperform because they ignore correlation. LDA-GO, by optimizing $\Sigma^{-1}$ directly and regularizing implicitly through gradient dynamics, is better equipped to exploit the dense signal while controlling overfitting.

\subsection*{Simulation 7: Sparse Signal Shift with Identity Covariance}
Let $p = 500$,  but only the first 5 contribute to class separation:
\[
X \mid Y = 2 \text{ has } +1 \text{ shift in first 5 entries}, \quad X \mid Y = 1 \sim \mathcal{N}(0, I).
\]
\textbf{Purpose:} To test scalability and feature selectivity. LDA-GO can overfit unless regularization is added.

\subsection*{Simulation 8: Sparse Separation Signal with Toeplitz-Correlated Structure}

Let $p = 500$, and generate a data where only the last 10 dimensions contain class-separating information:
\[
\mu_1 = \mathbf{0}, \quad \mu_2 = [\underbrace{0, \dots, 0}_{490}, \underbrace{1, \dots, 1}_{10}],
\]
and $\Sigma = \text{Toeplitz}(0.9^{|i-j|})$. Then 
\[
X \mid Y=1 \sim \mathcal{N}(\mu_1, \Sigma), \quad X \mid Y=2 \sim \mathcal{N}(\mu_2, \Sigma),
\]
This is designed to evaluate performance under high correlation and sparse signal, where covariance estimation is critical.

\subsection*{Simulation 9: Opposing Directions with Shared Covariance}
Let $p = 500$, and for $k=1, 2$ set
\[
X \mid Y = k \sim \mathcal{N}((-1)^{k+1} v, I + 0.9 vv^\top),
\]
where $v$ is a randomly generated length $p$ vector of unit-norm. 
This is a designed for classical LDA to fail, due to class-dependent mean directions within the same principal subspace. 


\subsection*{Simulation 10: Sparse Signal Separation with Heterogeneous Dimension Blocks}
Let $p = 500$, and generate dimensions via three blocks:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Informative Signal Block (Dimension 1–20):}  
    Each class has a distinct mean vector in this block:
    \[
    \mu_1^{(1)} = \mathbf{0}, \quad \mu_2^{(1)} = \mathbf{1}_{20},
    \]
    and covariance $\Sigma^{(1)} = I_{20}$. Then 
    \[
    X^{(1)} \mid Y=1 \sim \mathcal{N}(\mu_1^{(1)}, \Sigma^{(1)}), \quad X^{(3)} \mid Y=2 \sim \mathcal{N}(\mu_1^{(2)}, \Sigma^{(1)}),
    \]
    This block carries all the meaningful class-discriminative information.

    \item \textbf{Correlated Noise Block (Dimension 21–40): }  
    Both classes share the same distribution in this block:
    \[
    X^{(2)} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \Sigma^{(2)}), \quad \Sigma^{(2)}_{ij} = 0.8^{|i-j|}.
    \]
    This is a high-correlation Toeplitz structure, contributing no discriminative power.

    \item \textbf{Heteroscedastic Noise Block (Dimension 41–500): }  
    Both classes have zero mean, but differing diagonal covariances:
    \[
    X^{(3)} \mid Y=1 \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \Sigma_1^{(3)}), \quad X^{(3)} \mid Y=2 \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \Sigma_2^{(3)}),
    \]
    where:
    \[
    \Sigma_1^{(3)} = \operatorname{diag}(0.5 + \text{Uniform}(0,1)), \quad 
    \Sigma_2^{(3)} = \operatorname{diag}(1.5 + \text{Uniform}(0,1)).
    \]
    This block introduces class-dependent noise but no signal in the mean.
\end{itemize}
Then $X=[X^{(1)};X^{(2)};X^{(3)}]$ is the final concenated training data. This setting has a mixed covariance structure that do not fit LDA assumption, but nevertheless still a linear decision boundary.


\subsection*{Simulation 11: Radial Nonlinear Structure in High Dimensions}
\textbf{Description:}
\[
Y = 1 \Rightarrow X \text{ on sphere of radius 2}, \quad Y = 2 \Rightarrow X \text{ on sphere of radius 4},
\]
where $X \in \mathbb{R}^{500}$ and uniformly distributed on the respective spheres.
\textbf{Purpose:} A fundamentally nonlinear problem where class membership depends on distance from the origin. LDA-GO is limited by its linearity, while nonlinear classifiers (e.g., neural nets) perform better.


\subsection*{Simulation 12: XOR Structure in 2D}
\textbf{Description:}
Data are clustered around the four points $(\pm 1, \pm 1)$, with:
\[
Y = \begin{cases}
1 & \text{if } x_1 x_2 > 0, \\
2 & \text{otherwise.}
\end{cases}
\]
\textbf{Purpose:} This is a classic nonlinearly separable problem. LDA-GO, like LDA, fails due to its linear boundaries, while nonlinear classifiers succeed.

Note that we don't expect it to work well for nonlinear relationships in general.

bib.abstract:
This paper explores fear‐arousing content in Australian former Prime Minister John Howard's political rhetoric about terrorism. We coded 27 speeches delivered between September 2001 and November 2007 for the presence of statements promoting fear‐consistent appraisals (
Smith \& Lazarus, 1993
). Fear‐arousing content was present in 24 of these speeches, but the amount of fear‐arousing content varied markedly. In particular, rhetoric that raised doubts about the capacity of Australia and its allies to cope with terrorism was most strongly present in the lead‐up to the invasion of Iraq and at times of declining support for government policies. Textual analysis of three key speeches confirmed a marked difference between Howard's speech given immediately after the attacks on September 11, 2001, and the second and third speeches presented prior to and after the 2003 invasion of Iraq. These findings indicate that Howard has not consistently employed fear‐inducing rhetoric in his speeches about terrorism, but that particular speeches appear to take this form, raising the possibility that fear‐arousing rhetoric may have been selectively deployed to support his political purposes at those times.

