\subsection{Synthesibility Cost Guidance Ablations}
\subsubsection{Cost Prediction Model Ablation}
\label{app:cost_model_ablation}
In this section, we demonstrate the importance of accurately estimating the true cost $c$ using the machine learning model $c'$ for the optimal performance of \our{}. We compare our model with a low-quality cost estimator that outputs a constant value for every input molecule, where this constant is the mean of all the building blocks in the given scenario. It is important to note that we only modify the recursive component of the $C^S$ function from \Cref{eq:synthesis_cost_def}, keeping the fragment costs unchanged. As shown in \Cref{tab:cost_ablation_2_seh}, the use of a constant value can reduce costs and improve \our{}'s performance in MEDIUM and LARGE settings. However, machine learning-based cost estimation significantly outperforms this approach, providing a substantial performance boost, particularly in cost reduction. From this, we conclude that the quality of the cost prediction model used in Cost Guidance is critical to the performance of SCENT.

