\subsubsection{Comparison of Cost-Reducing Methods}
\label{app:cost_reduction_methods}
In this section, we compare our cost-guided \our{} with other methods to reduce the synthesis cost. The first method is to use the forward synthesis cost as a reward function component (we denote this approach as the "cost reward"). The second method that we tested is trained on subsets of the building block library $M$ with the top $n\%$ of the most expensive molecules being removed. While both approaches can effectively reduce the cost of proposed molecules, they lag behind cost-guided \our{} in terms of online discovery metrics, as can be seen in \Cref{fig:cost_ablation_medium}. This suggests that cost biasing strikes the perfect balance between cost-effectiveness and diversity as it is able to consistently find more modes.

